1
00:00:08,593 --> 00:00:10,761
Hi , I 'm Sigourney Weaver . I played Ripley .

2
00:00:10,928 --> 00:00:14,515
Hi , I 'm Ridley Scott , I 'm 32 years old , and I 'm the director of the movie .

3
00:00:14,682 --> 00:00:16,684
( Weaver laughs )

4
00:00:17,059 --> 00:00:19,729
Funny enough , on the way here this morning , Alan Ladd called me .

5
00:00:19,895 --> 00:00:21,981
- ( Weaver ) You 're kidding ? - ( Scott ) Yeah . And he called me yesterday ,

6
00:00:22,148 --> 00:00:24,775
so he obviously has some material .

7
00:00:24,942 --> 00:00:27,194
And Laddie was always ...

8
00:00:27,361 --> 00:00:32,658
It 's interesting cos he somehow gets his hands on some very interesting material ,

9
00:00:32,825 --> 00:00:34,744
and either develops it or otherwise .

10
00:00:34,910 --> 00:00:37,830
This one was when Alan was the head of the studio at 20th Century Fox

11
00:00:37,997 --> 00:00:43,377
and he was enjoying a tremendously successful run of everything , which would ...

12
00:00:43,544 --> 00:00:46,464
You know , what leaps to mind is Star Wars , okay ?

13
00:00:46,631 --> 00:00:53,262
That was his first Star Wars . And so Laddie was in full glow here .

14
00:00:53,429 --> 00:00:56,390
I 'm Ron Shusett . I 'm executive producer of Alien ,

15
00:00:56,557 --> 00:00:59,852
and also cowrote the original story with Dan O 'Bannon .

16
00:01:00,019 --> 00:01:04,732
The title sequence took us a long time . We first tried it ...

17
00:01:04,899 --> 00:01:11,072
The first concept , I think , was bits of flesh and bone

18
00:01:11,238 --> 00:01:13,491
were forming the words " Alien . "

19
00:01:13,658 --> 00:01:17,578
And we decided that was too gory . It was too classy for that .

20
00:01:17,745 --> 00:01:19,372
I don 't know who came up with the concept

21
00:01:19,538 --> 00:01:23,376
of the letters slowly building and generating till it said " Alien , "

22
00:01:23,542 --> 00:01:27,421
but it was certainly a great forward for the movie .

23
00:01:27,588 --> 00:01:31,175
It gave it a really classy quality .

24
00:01:32,218 --> 00:01:37,431
( Scott ) The titles were something which is always a difficult thing to do ,

25
00:01:37,598 --> 00:01:39,475
and it 's always the last thing to get considered .

26
00:01:39,642 --> 00:01:42,770
But because I 'd started talking about the marketing on this fairly early on ...

27
00:01:42,937 --> 00:01:45,022
Cos we figured we 're gonna go out ...

28
00:01:45,189 --> 00:01:47,942
You know , we knew pretty soon where we gonna go out .

29
00:01:48,109 --> 00:01:51,195
We were gonna go out in one of the peak moments of the year , which is May .

30
00:01:51,362 --> 00:01:55,199
So we 'd already started talking to the designers

31
00:01:55,366 --> 00:01:57,702
who would actually come up with the poster ,

32
00:01:57,868 --> 00:02:00,121
and came up with a beautiful logo .

33
00:02:00,287 --> 00:02:04,542
So then I asked them if they would then consider taking that particular logo -

34
00:02:04,709 --> 00:02:06,961
the " Alien , " the word " Alien " -

35
00:02:07,128 --> 00:02:10,172
and incorporating it into the front of the film .

36
00:02:10,339 --> 00:02:12,174
And they said " Well , what do you want ? "

37
00:02:12,341 --> 00:02:16,971
I said " Well , somehow , it should come off as a hieroglyphic or a readout . "

38
00:02:17,138 --> 00:02:20,683
" So you don 't quite know what it is as it 's coming up and being spelt out . "

39
00:02:20,850 --> 00:02:22,351
And that 's what they did . And I ...

40
00:02:22,518 --> 00:02:28,858
It 's still one of the best logo title cards I 've ever had ,

41
00:02:29,024 --> 00:02:31,193
you know , for any of the films I 've done .

42
00:02:31,360 --> 00:02:33,112
Beautifully done and intriguing ,

43
00:02:33,279 --> 00:02:36,157
and somehow absolutely appropriate for the film .

44
00:02:36,323 --> 00:02:39,827
Hello , this is John Hurt . I play Kane .

45
00:02:39,994 --> 00:02:42,121
Well , here we are .

46
00:02:42,288 --> 00:02:45,416
These are one of the shots , of course , that we 're so used to at the time

47
00:02:45,583 --> 00:02:51,505
because of the other space films , you know ? The ... Star Wars , that sort of thing .

48
00:02:51,672 --> 00:02:54,175
But of course they were all spanking new , and things like that .

49
00:02:54,341 --> 00:02:56,385
Whereas , one of the things that Ridley wanted to get ,

50
00:02:56,552 --> 00:03:00,848
was the feeling of , really , an old , battered old ship

51
00:03:01,015 --> 00:03:05,561
that had been bashing around the planets for donkey 's years .

52
00:03:05,728 --> 00:03:11,192
( Scott ) So here we are inside our retro industrial corridors ,

53
00:03:11,358 --> 00:03:15,654
which were fundamentally made up of remains of aircraft

54
00:03:15,821 --> 00:03:18,741
that we found in aircraft graveyards .

55
00:03:18,908 --> 00:03:21,368
None of the things we could really afford . Vacuum molding

56
00:03:21,535 --> 00:03:23,412
or presses or anything like that .

57
00:03:23,579 --> 00:03:26,123
And therefore , a lot of the stuff was found

58
00:03:26,290 --> 00:03:31,879
and then assembled like sculpture and then painted .

59
00:03:32,046 --> 00:03:38,177
Then joined together with nicely designed door architraves in polystyrene ,

60
00:03:38,344 --> 00:03:41,430
and sprayed to look like plastic .

61
00:03:41,597 --> 00:03:46,268
You know , I was very conscious of the set and the condition of the set ,

62
00:03:46,435 --> 00:03:49,688
as to whether it looked aged enough .

63
00:03:49,855 --> 00:03:52,566
( Shusett ) And Ron Cobb designed a lot of these earth interiors .

64
00:03:52,733 --> 00:03:56,821
He had never worked on a movie before , except a low-budget movie

65
00:03:56,987 --> 00:03:59,698
which Dan O 'Bannon discovered him in called Dark Star ,

66
00:03:59,865 --> 00:04:02,660
that was done as Dan 's thesis at film school .

67
00:04:02,827 --> 00:04:05,746
All these interiors were Ron Cobb 's - the human interiors -

68
00:04:05,913 --> 00:04:09,333
and the alien interiors were by Giger mostly .

69
00:04:09,500 --> 00:04:12,628
( Scott ) The ideas of the helmets came , again , late in the day .

70
00:04:12,795 --> 00:04:17,341
What I developed this into is I put two helmets on the back of the seats ,

71
00:04:17,508 --> 00:04:22,429
developed some little 16-millimeter projectors that simply projected onto the helmets

72
00:04:22,596 --> 00:04:29,353
as if it were the small monitors relaying alphanumerics and conversation

73
00:04:29,520 --> 00:04:33,274
between the two computers . So these two computers are chatting .

74
00:04:34,942 --> 00:04:40,114
Well , I 'm Dan O 'Bannon and I wrote the film Alien .

75
00:04:40,281 --> 00:04:42,491
I remember that little corridor we 're looking at now .

76
00:04:42,658 --> 00:04:45,119
They built that at my insistence .

77
00:04:45,286 --> 00:04:48,455
All of the corridors they were building in the spaceship were straight .

78
00:04:48,622 --> 00:04:52,459
They were laid down in a grid . And I said " There are no blind corners . "

79
00:04:52,626 --> 00:04:55,838
" You need some blind corners in your spaceship . "

80
00:04:56,005 --> 00:04:57,923
And they built the thing , and Ridley ended up

81
00:04:58,090 --> 00:05:02,344
putting the hyper-sleep vault at the end of it .

82
00:05:02,595 --> 00:05:04,763
Very nicely handled , the way when the door opens

83
00:05:04,930 --> 00:05:09,768
that the little coat hanging by the door moves slightly .

84
00:05:09,935 --> 00:05:15,482
( Scott ) Little details like this , where you get negative air when that door opens .

85
00:05:15,649 --> 00:05:22,573
There 's the negative air which is protecting them inside from bacteria .

86
00:05:22,740 --> 00:05:28,495
And it 's all not really scientifically thought through . It 's viscerally thought through

87
00:05:28,662 --> 00:05:30,998
because I 'm too much of a logician , that 's the problem .

88
00:05:31,165 --> 00:05:36,670
And I have never quite bought yet into the notion of cryogenics ,

89
00:05:36,837 --> 00:05:38,505
in terms of its possibilities .

90
00:05:38,672 --> 00:05:43,719
I 'm sure it may one day happen , but I think we 're a long way off .

91
00:05:44,637 --> 00:05:48,807
If you think about it carefully , it doesn 't make sense ,

92
00:05:48,974 --> 00:05:52,019
but I think we got away with it .

93
00:05:52,186 --> 00:05:54,855
And Jerry , particularly , helped here

94
00:05:55,022 --> 00:05:58,692
with one of the best parts of the score in the film .

95
00:05:58,859 --> 00:06:01,612
In that waking sequence , I really love that .

96
00:06:01,779 --> 00:06:05,574
Somehow helped to convey everything ,

97
00:06:05,741 --> 00:06:08,535
