229
00:12:57,611 --> 00:13:01,907
He 'd set up several cameras - of the scenes that come up later , I 'll tell you .

230
00:13:02,074 --> 00:13:07,788
But he trusted us well . He loved to get impromptu moments .

231
00:13:07,954 --> 00:13:12,959
And he did that by having more than one camera working pretty well all the time .

232
00:13:13,126 --> 00:13:16,046
So he had angles well covered

233
00:13:16,213 --> 00:13:21,134
and things that would have a documentary feel to it , in a sense .

234
00:13:24,971 --> 00:13:28,475
( Scott ) The difference from the original cut is probably just over 12 minutes ,

235
00:13:28,642 --> 00:13:33,021
12 and a half minutes of material that hasn 't really been seen before .

236
00:13:33,188 --> 00:13:36,983
The reason why it was taken out was basically about story dynamics .

237
00:13:37,150 --> 00:13:40,529
And there 's a certain point when you have the story ,

238
00:13:40,695 --> 00:13:43,115
and therefore the dynamics - particularly on a film like this ,

239
00:13:43,281 --> 00:13:51,123
which is fundamentally a thriller - really moving at the impetus it was going .

240
00:13:51,289 --> 00:13:55,919
You suddenly didn 't want to have something , or scene where it took a minute out

241
00:13:56,086 --> 00:14:00,132
or two minutes out to basically work against that impetus .

242
00:14:00,298 --> 00:14:03,802
And so that 's why the new stuff that you see

243
00:14:03,969 --> 00:14:08,932
ended up back in the editing room , not going into the film .

244
00:14:09,099 --> 00:14:12,519
But it 's quite interesting to revisit this after all these years ,

245
00:14:12,686 --> 00:14:15,772
to see maybe I should 've left it in . Heh .

246
00:14:23,029 --> 00:14:27,701
( Hurt ) Well , I understand that I was intended to be in the film originally ,

247
00:14:27,868 --> 00:14:29,995
but I was , at that time , not available .

248
00:14:30,162 --> 00:14:33,373
And I was going to be doing a film in South Africa .

249
00:14:33,540 --> 00:14:36,293
But I wasn 't allowed to go to South Africa .

250
00:14:36,460 --> 00:14:40,255
And then , of course , we had to try and find out what the reason was .

251
00:14:40,422 --> 00:14:43,842
It turned out , in the end , that I was probably confused

252
00:14:44,009 --> 00:14:48,138
with that very wonderful actor John Heard , who was a political activist

253
00:14:48,305 --> 00:14:53,351
and had gone down on the South African books as an undesirable

254
00:14:53,518 --> 00:14:56,271
because he didn 't believe in apartheid .

255
00:14:56,438 --> 00:14:59,900
Well , none of us agreed with apartheid ,

256
00:15:00,066 --> 00:15:04,196
but fortunately some of us were not on the books at that particular time .

257
00:15:04,362 --> 00:15:06,156
So I came back , I didn 't do that .

258
00:15:06,323 --> 00:15:12,120
And then Jon Finch - who was going to be playing Kane in Alien - became sick .

259
00:15:12,287 --> 00:15:17,792
And so therefore , the inquiry came in again and I was available this time .

260
00:15:17,959 --> 00:15:21,296
And I remember Ridley coming around to talk to me .

261
00:15:21,463 --> 00:15:26,510
And we were talking till 12 : 00 at night - and he was pitching it for me -

262
00:15:26,676 --> 00:15:32,182
and at 7 : 00 that morning , I was on the set . So it all happened very quickly .

263
00:15:32,349 --> 00:15:37,312
( Scott ) The idea of a giant magnetic clamp - seeing it drop away -

264
00:15:37,771 --> 00:15:40,440
is , I guess , fair game , why not ?

265
00:15:40,607 --> 00:15:44,653
You get the thing , you wouldn 't see flame , you wouldn 't get vapor ,

266
00:15:44,819 --> 00:15:48,114
and eventually you just say " Oh , shut up , " you know .

267
00:15:48,281 --> 00:15:51,660
I always loved the cue here in relation to the rolling craft .

268
00:15:51,826 --> 00:15:56,831
I wanted the roll synced to the graphic , as the graphic rolls you as well .

269
00:15:56,998 --> 00:16:01,670
Logic of that ... Thank you , Stanley . Very good score .

270
00:16:01,836 --> 00:16:06,299
This is Jerry 's ... Really at his best , I think , in this film .

271
00:16:06,466 --> 00:16:09,803
He may not agree , but I think it is . It 's really great .

272
00:16:09,970 --> 00:16:13,807
My name 's Terry Rawlings . I was the editor of Alien .

273
00:16:13,974 --> 00:16:16,643
Goldsmith wrote such a great score for this film ,

274
00:16:16,810 --> 00:16:19,604
which is a bone of contention between the two of us .

275
00:16:19,771 --> 00:16:25,777
Cos I think he 's a genius , but we didn 't agree on everything .

276
00:16:27,696 --> 00:16:29,781
I put music in the film before we get the composer on

277
00:16:29,948 --> 00:16:33,868
because of screenings we have to have and previews .

278
00:16:34,035 --> 00:16:36,371
And I went out of my way to temp it with Jerry Goldsmith

279
00:16:36,538 --> 00:16:38,331
because I knew he was gonna do the score .

280
00:16:38,498 --> 00:16:42,460
And one of the sections - the air shafts -

281
00:16:42,627 --> 00:16:46,381
I used his music from the film Freud that he did ,

282
00:16:46,548 --> 00:16:51,052
which they liked better than what we finished up by getting from him for this film ,

283
00:16:51,219 --> 00:16:53,388
which upset him terribly .

284
00:16:53,555 --> 00:16:57,225
And the end of the film , which is Howard Hanson 's " Symphony No. 2 , "

285
00:16:57,392 --> 00:16:58,810
was so perfect for it .

286
00:16:58,977 --> 00:17:03,857
It just did something that Jerry 's end titles didn 't achieve ,

287
00:17:04,024 --> 00:17:05,650
even though it was good .

288
00:17:05,817 --> 00:17:09,779
It just didn 't give us that emotional content .

289
00:17:11,072 --> 00:17:13,241
( Scott ) The experts are saying " There is no atmosphere . "

290
00:17:13,408 --> 00:17:18,496
I said " There is in this film . Otherwise , my model looked like ... Not good . "

291
00:17:22,709 --> 00:17:24,419
And this is waggling seats ,

292
00:17:24,586 --> 00:17:27,756
where I 've got a guy crouched down there wobbling the seats ,

293
00:17:27,922 --> 00:17:32,344
which is driving everyone crazy , and you get eyeball rolling ...

294
00:17:33,428 --> 00:17:37,140
And you 've just gotta stick to your guns . I 'd have liked more wobbling .

295
00:17:37,307 --> 00:17:40,143
I 'd have liked impossible shuddering and shaking ,

296
00:17:40,310 --> 00:17:42,062
but we weren 't designed for that .

297
00:17:42,228 --> 00:17:46,358
Every step you make , everybody 's doubting Thomas , you know .

298
00:17:46,524 --> 00:17:48,777
But that 's where you gotta earn your way .

299
00:17:48,943 --> 00:17:52,822
But I just wonder how many people fall by the wayside

300
00:17:52,989 --> 00:17:59,120
because they can 't push their point home and don 't quite get what they want .

301
00:17:59,287 --> 00:18:04,084
Nobody respects you later for having been a nice guy and given up .

302
00:18:04,250 --> 00:18:07,045
You gotta get it . You have to get it now

303
00:18:07,212 --> 00:18:11,257
because you 're gonna wear what you got , basically .

304
00:18:11,716 --> 00:18:17,472
You can be very unpopular on the route , but if you 're right , all is forgiven .

305
00:18:18,973 --> 00:18:20,475
( O 'Bannon ) Now , this sequence here -

306
00:18:20,642 --> 00:18:24,020
when the spaceship is landing on the planetoid -

307
00:18:24,187 --> 00:18:27,440
this is one of the scenes in the movie that came the closest

308
00:18:27,607 --> 00:18:30,777
to the way that I imagined it when I was writing it .

309
00:18:31,277 --> 00:18:35,657
In science-fiction movies up to that time , when a spaceship landed on a planet

310
00:18:35,824 --> 00:18:39,452
it was usually depicted as a pretty effortless endeavor .

311
00:18:39,619 --> 00:18:43,289
The ship would float down from space and , poof , it would land .

312
00:18:43,456 --> 00:18:46,543
When I came to write this , I started thinking about

313
00:18:46,710 --> 00:18:49,546
the times that I 'd been a passenger on a big airliner

314
00:18:49,713 --> 00:18:52,966
when they were landing and taking off - particularly during turbulence -

315
00:18:53,133 --> 00:18:57,679
and the way that the aircraft would groan and rattle and twist

316
00:18:57,846 --> 00:19:01,182
and , you know , bump and clatter .

317
00:19:01,349 --> 00:19:06,646
And I thought that it would be really a novel and , you know , an interesting effect

318
00:19:06,813 --> 00:19:08,982
if you could show that landing through the atmosphere

319
00:19:09,149 --> 00:19:12,026
to be a horrendous and dangerous process

320
00:19:12,193 --> 00:19:15,864
where you didn 't really know if , maybe , the ship would be torn apart

321
00:19:16,030 --> 00:19:18,742
before it ever made it to the surface of the planet .

322
00:19:18,908 --> 00:19:24,706
And when I saw the film ... This sequence as it is in the movie now

323
00:19:24,873 --> 00:19:28,334
with the Nostromo coming down through that dense atmosphere

324
00:19:28,501 --> 00:19:31,379
and wrenching and groaning and crashing ,

325
00:19:31,546 --> 00:19:34,466
was really just very close to what I had imagined .

326
00:19:34,632 --> 00:19:37,385
I was very gratified by that .

327
00:19:38,052 --> 00:19:40,972
( Scott ) And again , we started to get full use of ...

328
00:19:41,139 --> 00:19:43,600
What they had in those days was scissor arcs ,

329
00:19:43,767 --> 00:19:45,477
which are basically lightning effects .

330
00:19:45,643 --> 00:19:47,520
Which drove the sound men crazy cos you ...

331
00:19:47,687 --> 00:19:51,733
It basically means you 're bringing two arcs together and they splutter .

332
00:19:51,983 --> 00:19:54,736
So that 's scissor arcs going in the background .

333
00:19:55,737 --> 00:19:56,738
( Scott chuckles )

334
00:19:56,905 --> 00:19:59,407
These two are really funny . They 're very real , these two .

335
00:19:59,574 --> 00:20:02,786
I thought they were great . Like , two guys on an oil rig , you know ?

336
00:20:02,952 --> 00:20:08,416
That 's , again , a model with an insert of a teeny back-projection screen

337
00:20:08,583 --> 00:20:12,086
about a foot and a half by , maybe , 6 inches .

338
00:20:12,253 --> 00:20:14,714
And I 'd photographed them on 16 ,

339
00:20:14,881 --> 00:20:19,093
then projected on the screen , interlocked and photographed it .

340
00:20:19,260 --> 00:20:20,887
Pretty primitive .

341
00:20:21,054 --> 00:20:24,474
Today that would be a snap . You wouldn 't even think about it .

342
00:20:24,724 --> 00:20:28,019
But again , coming back , having them in the window , gives you the size of the craft .

343
00:20:28,186 --> 00:20:31,481
You can see there the only thing left on at night in the craft was the little ...

344
00:20:31,648 --> 00:20:37,028
The nose light . The bay window in the control room .

345
00:20:37,821 --> 00:20:40,949
( O 'Bannon ) I mean , you gotta admit Ridley is a master of atmosphere .

346
00:20:41,115 --> 00:20:43,576
And when it comes to texturing a scene -

347
00:20:43,743 --> 00:20:48,122
texture , mood , subtlety of mood , and feeling and atmosphere -

348
00:20:48,289 --> 00:20:51,209
he really is superb .

349
00:20:51,376 --> 00:20:54,003
I mean , without it , it would have been a much lesser picture .

350
00:20:54,170 --> 00:20:59,008
When you 're doing a scary movie , a horror story , a suspense movie ,

351
00:20:59,175 --> 00:21:04,472
naturally plot is important , it 's vital , but so is atmosphere .

352
00:21:04,639 --> 00:21:09,811
And a horror movie that is not atmospheric is not a pleasing thing to watch .

353
00:21:09,978 --> 00:21:13,773
And Alien needed it and he provided it .

354
00:21:14,816 --> 00:21:18,820
( Scott ) And again , the low-key performances

355
00:21:18,987 --> 00:21:23,032
give it a kind of reality , I think .

356
00:21:23,199 --> 00:21:27,161
This is business as usual . They have to do it . They gotta go in .

357
00:21:27,328 --> 00:21:30,707
She has a certain amount of trepidation , a little anxiety .

358
00:21:30,874 --> 00:21:33,418
Ash is obviously a scientist

359
00:21:33,585 --> 00:21:38,631
who would rather be tramping around an , you know , unfriendly landscape

360
00:21:38,798 --> 00:21:40,717
than going back to Earth .

361
00:21:40,884 --> 00:21:43,094
So he 's obviously got something going on then .

362
00:21:43,261 --> 00:21:48,808
Not that you know anything like that other than you 're registering his enthusiasm .

363
00:21:49,559 --> 00:21:52,186
First strange look at Ash .

364
00:21:52,353 --> 00:21:55,398
I always liked those blisters at the bottom of Blenheim -

365
00:21:55,565 --> 00:21:57,317
old bomber , Wellington bomber -

366
00:21:57,483 --> 00:21:59,360
and that 's where we put him , in his own blister .

367
00:21:59,527 --> 00:22:02,614
That funny little jog is a clue .

368
00:22:02,780 --> 00:22:05,867
Maybe all robots get stiff .

369
00:22:06,034 --> 00:22:11,039
Well , he 's not a robot , he 's a kind of humanoid biomechanoid ...

370
00:22:11,205 --> 00:22:16,169
You know , he 's a replicant , basically . Half human , two-thirds human .

371
00:22:17,211 --> 00:22:20,840
( Skerritt ) Okay , here we are in these things that had never been tried before ,

372
00:22:21,007 --> 00:22:25,219
and I remember I damn near suffocated in it .

373
00:22:25,386 --> 00:22:26,512
( Cartwright ) These were the headgear .

374
00:22:26,679 --> 00:22:29,766
It was like football gear but it weighed about 75 pounds .

375
00:22:29,933 --> 00:22:33,394
They told us they were gonna put oxygen ... A little air hole thing in it ,

376
00:22:33,561 --> 00:22:36,731
and instead , what would happen was the C2 canisters would leak

377
00:22:36,898 --> 00:22:38,816
and we 'd see these lovely little swirls of smoke .

378
00:22:38,983 --> 00:22:41,778
And we 'd go " I don 't know . I 'm seeing smoke .

379
00:22:41,945 --> 00:22:44,781
And he would " That 's impossible , just impossible . "

380
00:22:44,948 --> 00:22:50,453
And then when we had to lug John across the desert and then take him up ...

381
00:22:50,620 --> 00:22:54,582
And we had these hockey gloves that had so much paint on them , they wouldn 't move .

382
00:22:54,749 --> 00:22:57,835
We didn 't have any sound , we had no air , and I start to pass out .

383
00:22:58,002 --> 00:23:01,923
And you 're waving your arms up there going : And I 'm like ...

384
00:23:02,090 --> 00:23:03,341
( Skerritt ) Yeah , I started to pass out too .

385
00:23:03,508 --> 00:23:05,093
( Cartwright ) And we 're like 24 feet up in the air .

386
00:23:05,259 --> 00:23:10,014
John Hurt , he had to have an oxygen tank every time he went out on this thing .

387
00:23:10,181 --> 00:23:12,600
( Hurt ) And there was a moment when the plumes -

388
00:23:12,767 --> 00:23:17,647
that are on our ... You know , that were done with a sort of aerosol can -

389
00:23:19,190 --> 00:23:22,610
came out of the top of the thing . You 'll see them shortly .

390
00:23:23,069 --> 00:23:25,446
They were actually leaking into our helmets .

391
00:23:25,613 --> 00:23:27,907
They said that they ... You know , the helmets couldn 't leak

392
00:23:28,074 --> 00:23:31,035
but there was a tube in it which had to bend slightly , and it broke .

393
00:23:31,202 --> 00:23:36,499
And Veronica and I nearly fainted because the aerosol was getting into the ...

394
00:23:36,666 --> 00:23:39,502
Which is quite poisonous . Into the helmet itself .

395
00:23:39,669 --> 00:23:44,173
" No , no , no , that 's not possible " they said . But it was , I can assure you .

396
00:23:46,467 --> 00:23:48,761
( Scott ) My most useful special effect in the corridors

397
00:23:48,928 --> 00:23:52,640
became dry ice in the tube .

398
00:23:52,807 --> 00:23:54,308
And the problem is

399
00:23:54,475 --> 00:23:57,770
when you run it for any length of time , it sucks the oxygen out of the air ,

400
00:23:57,937 --> 00:24:01,315
so at the end of a take you start to get out of breath .

401
00:24:01,983 --> 00:24:07,780
We were assured it was entirely safe . So I said " Okay , let 's use ' em . "

402
00:24:15,038 --> 00:24:18,332
Rocks , which were smooth and weatherworn .

403
00:24:18,499 --> 00:24:20,543
I always loved the spacesuits , I think they 're great .

404
00:24:20,710 --> 00:24:23,504
And they ... We had a big problem with the space helmets

405
00:24:23,671 --> 00:24:27,341
because once you 're inside that helmet , just your body heat and body temperature

406
00:24:27,508 --> 00:24:30,720
starts to send up the temperature inside the helmet .

407
00:24:30,887 --> 00:24:35,099
And therefore , you either form condensation or you get short of breath ,

408
00:24:35,266 --> 00:24:37,393
and then you get panicked because you 've got no breath .

409
00:24:37,560 --> 00:24:39,854
And some people are claustrophobic and some people aren 't .

410
00:24:40,021 --> 00:24:42,648
So we had quite a lot of problems with that .

411
00:24:42,815 --> 00:24:45,401
And with nonstop complaints from the thespians .

412
00:24:45,568 --> 00:24:48,613
But then I don 't blame them because I didn 't have to wear them , they did .

413
00:24:48,780 --> 00:24:51,365
But , you know , we didn 't have the technology and money

414
00:24:51,532 --> 00:24:57,538
to run air lines into those suits and into the helmets , like you do today .

415
00:24:57,705 --> 00:25:03,753
Like , you know , I think Jim could design his own helmet on the Abyss

416
00:25:04,003 --> 00:25:05,880
twelve years later , and literally ,

417
00:25:06,255 --> 00:25:10,176
have some manufacturer make the headsets and the face masks ,

418
00:25:10,343 --> 00:25:13,513
and they 're just walking along breathing happily off oxygen .

419
00:25:13,679 --> 00:25:18,184
You 'd think we could do that in those days . No , it wasn 't ... We couldn 't afford it .

420
00:25:18,643 --> 00:25:21,562
So all of this is shot in one stage . You know , being careful

421
00:25:21,729 --> 00:25:24,273
about showing your angles , getting close up . Cos you 're real ...

422
00:25:24,565 --> 00:25:27,860
Your real investment here is in the ... You know , in the ...

423
00:25:28,027 --> 00:25:30,613
For hardwares and the suits , which are beautiful .

424
00:25:30,780 --> 00:25:33,032
So a little clump of rocks ...

425
00:25:33,199 --> 00:25:36,285
I just kept circling the rocks until I was ready to go ...

426
00:25:36,452 --> 00:25:40,414
That 's the clump of rocks and that light over there in the background -

427
00:25:40,581 --> 00:25:45,253
filled with smoke - looks real to me , on a big screen particularly .

428
00:25:45,419 --> 00:25:50,424
( O 'Bannon ) It really looks like the sun setting behind a stormy atmosphere .

429
00:25:51,175 --> 00:25:55,096
His understanding and command of how to achieve lighting effects

430
00:25:55,263 --> 00:25:59,851
was so good that he was able to achieve that with great simplicity .

431
00:26:00,226 --> 00:26:01,769
- ( Weaver ) Look how beautiful it is . - ( Scott ) Yeah .

432
00:26:01,936 --> 00:26:07,775
You see , all that stuff is one pass done with artwork and simplicity ,

433
00:26:07,942 --> 00:26:09,694
in terms of your effects .

434
00:26:09,861 --> 00:26:13,781
This was done because I had to do it cos the set was not very good .

435
00:26:13,948 --> 00:26:16,242
The set was only a foot high .

436
00:26:16,409 --> 00:26:18,452
These rocks were about a foot and half high .

437
00:26:18,619 --> 00:26:21,455
And I walked in Bray and I hadn 't had time to go and see it .

438
00:26:21,622 --> 00:26:25,293
Saw the set and went " Oh , God . " And we just stood there .

439
00:26:25,459 --> 00:26:29,463
You know , necessity is really , really the mother of invention .

440
00:26:29,630 --> 00:26:32,466
And I sat there staring at it thinking " What the heck are you gonna do ? "

441
00:26:32,633 --> 00:26:35,761
Cos Peter Voysey 's sculpture was beautiful .

442
00:26:35,928 --> 00:26:37,430
And I said " Has anyone got ... ? "

443
00:26:37,597 --> 00:26:40,641
And I think it must have been very early tape camera .

444
00:26:40,808 --> 00:26:42,476
" Who 's got a tape camera ? "

445
00:26:42,643 --> 00:26:44,937
Said " Well , I 've got one at home . " " Go get it . "

446
00:26:45,104 --> 00:26:48,065
So what I did is ... I knew I couldn 't film it .

447
00:26:48,232 --> 00:26:50,902
First of all , I couldn 't get a camera - a Panaflex - down that low ,

448
00:26:51,068 --> 00:26:56,407
which it weighs 65 pounds , and move around like , you know , a hand-held camera ,

449
00:26:56,574 --> 00:26:59,869
so I simply got a domestic tape camera

450
00:27:00,036 --> 00:27:04,290
and walked through the set like this , putting that in the background ,

451
00:27:04,457 --> 00:27:08,753
then put it back through a TV monitor and filmed off the monitor .

452
00:27:08,920 --> 00:27:13,549
So that 's why , somehow , it looks high-tech and certainly has this massive scale .

453
00:27:13,716 --> 00:27:15,343
And that was because I had to do it .

454
00:27:15,509 --> 00:27:17,178
- ( Weaver ) Well , it 's brilliant . - ( Scott ) I had no choice .

455
00:27:17,345 --> 00:27:18,638
- ( Weaver ) It 's fantastic . - ( Scott ) Yeah .

456
00:27:18,804 --> 00:27:22,516
( Weaver ) Because also the fact that it keeps going in and out

457
00:27:22,683 --> 00:27:25,353
makes you so frightened that they won 't be able to see something .

458
00:27:25,519 --> 00:27:28,689
( Scott ) And the great sounds of you guys talking back .

459
00:27:28,856 --> 00:27:30,483
Talk back , talk back .

460
00:27:30,650 --> 00:27:35,112
Which also makes it a little bit more fraught and tense .

461
00:27:35,279 --> 00:27:39,033
And aids frustration about only getting half the transmission .

462
00:27:39,200 --> 00:27:45,289
And that 's where also Giger 's illustrations are fantastic .

463
00:27:45,456 --> 00:27:47,708
But when they 're translated literally ,

464
00:27:47,875 --> 00:27:51,003
sometimes they can be too much fantasy

465
00:27:51,170 --> 00:27:54,799
and not enough sense of reality , but ...

466
00:27:54,966 --> 00:27:57,635
So I was always a little anxious about the exterior .

467
00:27:57,802 --> 00:28:00,554
But then once we got inside , I felt far more comfortable

468
00:28:00,721 --> 00:28:04,058
because they 'd really dealt with this very well . This is a great shot here .

469
00:28:04,225 --> 00:28:11,357
Les Dilley 's set of Giger 's sculptural models and drawings .

470
00:28:11,524 --> 00:28:13,234
But , you know , see how well that 's done .

471
00:28:13,401 --> 00:28:16,737
That could easily have been very , very corny .

472
00:28:16,904 --> 00:28:21,242
But there 's something very organic and interesting - unique actually - about it .

473
00:28:21,409 --> 00:28:23,327
But I still think it 's probably one of the better

474
00:28:23,494 --> 00:28:28,207
of these sci-fi genre beast movies .

475
00:28:28,374 --> 00:28:31,585
The lights on the helmets helped tremendously

476
00:28:31,752 --> 00:28:33,963
because you get nice flare .

477
00:28:34,130 --> 00:28:36,590
So half the time it 's what you think you see .

478
00:28:36,757 --> 00:28:37,758
I , again , get this all ...

479
00:28:37,925 --> 00:28:41,470
If you 'd shot this clean , it would have looked like a set .

480
00:28:41,637 --> 00:28:44,390
But we have the flare , which helps a lot .

481
00:28:44,557 --> 00:28:47,560
( Skerritt ) And that was really awkward walking .

482
00:28:48,561 --> 00:28:49,687
- ( Cartwright ) Oh , it really was . - ( Skerritt ) Yeah .

483
00:28:49,854 --> 00:28:52,315
( Cartwright ) Those moon boots . We had moon boots on . And the suit .

484
00:28:52,481 --> 00:28:55,693
( Skerritt ) We 're trying to look so cool walking around in there , ready to fall at any time .

485
00:28:55,860 --> 00:28:57,320
( Cartwright ) We 're sweating like pigs .

486
00:28:57,486 --> 00:28:58,779
( commentators laughing )

487
00:28:58,946 --> 00:29:01,782
And those masks kept fogging up .

488
00:29:01,949 --> 00:29:03,701
- Remember how they 'd fog up ? - ( Skerritt ) Oh , yeah .

489
00:29:03,868 --> 00:29:05,745
( Cartwright ) You couldn 't even see John half the time .

490
00:29:05,911 --> 00:29:07,371
( Skerritt ) Oh , boy .

491
00:29:07,580 --> 00:29:08,914
( commentators laughing )

492
00:29:09,915 --> 00:29:13,169
( Stanton ) They couldn 't get the ... They didn 't have the fog thing worked out , huh ?

493
00:29:13,336 --> 00:29:15,463
( Cartwright ) No , they 'd forgotten to put any escape .

494
00:29:15,629 --> 00:29:17,256
( Stanton ) We can send men to the moon ,

495
00:29:17,423 --> 00:29:19,800
and we can 't even figure out a simple thing like that .

496
00:29:19,967 --> 00:29:24,472
( Cartwright ) And see those hockey gloves ? They were really hard to manipulate .

497
00:29:27,016 --> 00:29:32,772
( O 'Bannon ) Ridley - when he shot the film - he went to a great deal of trouble

498
00:29:33,147 --> 00:29:40,279
to create a lighting situation which would duplicate the look of Giger 's paintings .

499
00:29:40,446 --> 00:29:42,156
One of the things he did , for example ,

500
00:29:42,323 --> 00:29:50,331
was to fill the entire set with a dense but uniform smoke .

501
00:29:50,498 --> 00:29:54,794
And he made sure that that smoke was uniformly distributed .

502
00:29:54,960 --> 00:30:00,299
He didn 't want billowing clouds , what he wanted was atmosphere ,

503
00:30:00,674 --> 00:30:02,843
was a thickness of the air .

504
00:30:03,010 --> 00:30:09,350
He had people walking around with incense burners filling the stage with smoke ,

505
00:30:09,517 --> 00:30:12,103
and then he personally walked around through the sound stage

506
00:30:12,269 --> 00:30:15,606
with a big piece of cardboard , waving it and waving it .

507
00:30:15,773 --> 00:30:19,610
And I couldn 't quite figure out what he was doing .

508
00:30:19,777 --> 00:30:21,404
But he didn 't shoot

509
00:30:21,570 --> 00:30:26,867
until the fog was uniformly distributed throughout the entire set

510
00:30:27,034 --> 00:30:32,915
so that you couldn 't see it billowing at all . It simply thickened the air .

511
00:30:33,082 --> 00:30:37,670
And once he had that , he lit it with elaborate care .

512
00:30:37,837 --> 00:30:42,174
He didn 't just put one or two big lights on everything and be satisfied with it .

513
00:30:42,341 --> 00:30:45,177
I didn 't understand half of what he was doing .

514
00:30:45,344 --> 00:30:49,056
He ran around clipping all these little snoot lights onto things -

515
00:30:49,223 --> 00:30:52,059
where you wouldn 't see them - and aiming them at different objects

516
00:30:52,226 --> 00:30:55,521
and different parts of the set and different props .

517
00:30:55,688 --> 00:30:58,274
And elaborate and careful lighting ,

518
00:30:58,441 --> 00:31:02,736
and filtering the color of the light toward a certain blue-gray tone

519
00:31:02,903 --> 00:31:08,200
in order to get the end photographic result to resemble Giger 's paintings .

520
00:31:08,742 --> 00:31:10,411
And if he had not done that ,

521
00:31:10,578 --> 00:31:13,706
I don 't think you would have gotten but a fraction of the value

522
00:31:13,873 --> 00:31:17,084
of Giger 's designs from all of that .

523
00:31:17,251 --> 00:31:21,422
( Hurt ) Yes , Giger was ... He was kind of bleakly fascinating

524
00:31:21,589 --> 00:31:25,426
with his scowls on the top of his four-poster beds and so on .

525
00:31:25,593 --> 00:31:28,596
And he was always dressed in black , as was his girlfriend ,

526
00:31:28,762 --> 00:31:32,850
which at that time was more unusual than it is now .

527
00:31:33,017 --> 00:31:35,728
There were only three people I knew who were always dressed in black

528
00:31:35,895 --> 00:31:38,939
and that was Harold Pinter , Giger and his girlfriend .

529
00:31:39,106 --> 00:31:41,775
And my father , but then he was a clergyman .

530
00:31:46,614 --> 00:31:49,450
I think the space jockey is actually somehow the pilot ,

531
00:31:49,617 --> 00:31:51,952
and he 's part of a military operation -

532
00:31:52,119 --> 00:31:54,997
if that 's the word you wanna apply to his world -

533
00:31:55,164 --> 00:31:58,250
and therefore , this is probably some kind of carrier .

534
00:31:58,417 --> 00:32:05,966
A weapon carrier , a biological or biomechanoid carrier of lethal eggs ,

535
00:32:06,133 --> 00:32:13,057
inside of which are these small creatures that will actually fundamentally integrate -

536
00:32:13,224 --> 00:32:17,269
in a very aggressive way - into any society or any place it dropped .

537
00:32:17,436 --> 00:32:22,316
So if you land on a human being , you 'll have a resemblance to a human being .

538
00:32:22,483 --> 00:32:25,819
If it dropped on an ostrich , it would look like an ostrich .

539
00:32:25,986 --> 00:32:28,656
And there 's a fundamental connection in that way in nature

540
00:32:28,822 --> 00:32:31,575
because we 'd actually watched in the preparation for this ...

541
00:32:31,742 --> 00:32:35,496
Oxford Scientific had this interesting piece of footage

542
00:32:35,663 --> 00:32:38,415
where they 'd watched a slice of bark -

543
00:32:38,582 --> 00:32:42,503
which in our terms , to a human being , would be about 12 feet thick -

544
00:32:42,670 --> 00:32:47,424
and there 's a grub underneath the bark , between the bark and the tree .

545
00:32:47,591 --> 00:32:49,635
There 's always a space between bark and tree .

546
00:32:49,802 --> 00:32:52,304
Across the top of the bark is crawling an insect

547
00:32:52,471 --> 00:32:58,394
which passes over the grub , stops , backs up , and feels the grub is there .

548
00:32:58,561 --> 00:33:01,897
Let 's say , the equivalent of 8 feet below you .

549
00:33:02,064 --> 00:33:07,444
And it goes up on its hind legs , produces a needle from between its legs

550
00:33:07,611 --> 00:33:12,116
and drills through the bark and bull 's-eyes right into the grub

551
00:33:12,283 --> 00:33:14,285
and lays its seed .

552
00:33:14,451 --> 00:33:18,664
So that the grub becomes the host of the insect .

553
00:33:18,831 --> 00:33:23,711
And now , does ... ? What comes out of the union of the grub and that particular insect ?

554
00:33:23,877 --> 00:33:25,963
Does that become a version of both ?

555
00:33:26,130 --> 00:33:29,758
And that 's what we basically , you know , went along with .

556
00:33:31,510 --> 00:33:34,054
The man running the laser beams at this particular moment -

557
00:33:34,221 --> 00:33:36,557
who had been doing rock shows and experimental laser beams -

558
00:33:36,724 --> 00:33:39,435
was Anton Furst , who later became an art director

559
00:33:39,602 --> 00:33:41,937
and actually did films such as Batman .

560
00:33:42,104 --> 00:33:46,900
And Anton was great to work with , with his very small team ,

561
00:33:47,067 --> 00:33:52,489
and I was absolutely literally blown away by the effect of these beams .

562
00:33:52,656 --> 00:33:54,908
Cos , you know , we hadn 't seen it before , really .

563
00:33:55,075 --> 00:34:01,707
And I thought this would be very useful to me to create the skin , like a protection .

564
00:34:16,263 --> 00:34:18,974
See the drops going upwards ? They 're all going up .

565
00:34:19,141 --> 00:34:21,101
That 's cos the egg 's upside down .

566
00:34:21,268 --> 00:34:24,271
And then , those are my hands in the middle there , in a pair of rubber gloves ,

567
00:34:24,438 --> 00:34:27,316
doing the old flutter as the light comes up .

568
00:34:28,067 --> 00:34:30,110
Cos I said " We gotta have movement in the eggs . "

569
00:34:30,277 --> 00:34:32,112
So I had a pair of surgical gloves ,

570
00:34:32,279 --> 00:34:35,783
I just stuck my hands into the egg and backlit it

571
00:34:35,949 --> 00:34:39,620
and , you know , we 'd do a little flutter , flutter .

572
00:34:39,787 --> 00:34:41,705
There it is again , a pair of hands .

573
00:34:41,872 --> 00:34:44,833
And it 's got some liquid in there .

574
00:34:54,927 --> 00:34:58,222
I love the opening here . It 's got a steel hydraulic on it .

575
00:34:58,389 --> 00:35:02,810
So you know that is gonna ... That 's strong .

576
00:35:04,812 --> 00:35:06,063
This is always a great moment .

577
00:35:06,230 --> 00:35:09,316
And I used - actually here - real organic material .

578
00:35:09,483 --> 00:35:12,986
This was delivered every morning from an abattoir ,

579
00:35:13,153 --> 00:35:16,824
with steamed cattle and sheep parts

580
00:35:17,157 --> 00:35:18,701
from the slaughterhouse .

581
00:35:18,867 --> 00:35:21,954
And that lacy stuff , in fact , is called Nottingham lace ,

582
00:35:22,121 --> 00:35:27,918
and in fact , is the lining of - some people eat it - a cow 's stomach .

583
00:35:28,085 --> 00:35:35,050
And the hose that comes out , in fact , is an intestine of a sheep ,

584
00:35:35,217 --> 00:35:38,303
which they use to make sausages .

585
00:35:38,470 --> 00:35:40,639
I used it because it 's diaphanous .

586
00:35:40,806 --> 00:35:47,479
So to actually put an air line on it , it just behaves like that . It just whips .

587
00:35:47,646 --> 00:35:51,775
And that was all discovered on the day .

588
00:35:52,526 --> 00:35:56,530
( O 'Bannon ) I 'm over here in L.A. Giger 's over there in Switzerland .

589
00:35:56,697 --> 00:35:58,031
We 're communicating on the phone .

590
00:35:58,198 --> 00:36:01,952
20th Century Fox has finally sent him some money to get started .

591
00:36:02,119 --> 00:36:06,206
And I sat down and I wrote out some very simple parameters

592
00:36:06,707 --> 00:36:09,835
for what I wanted him to start designing .

593
00:36:10,002 --> 00:36:15,215
And I described in the simplest terms what the facehugger was to do .

594
00:36:15,382 --> 00:36:19,595
That it was to be a small , sort of octopus-like thing

595
00:36:19,762 --> 00:36:22,556
that would leap onto a person 's face ,

596
00:36:22,723 --> 00:36:25,851
wrap its tentacles around behind the person 's head ,

597
00:36:26,018 --> 00:36:28,729
and then it would have an organ - an ovipositor -

598
00:36:28,896 --> 00:36:31,315
which it would shove down the person 's throat .

599
00:36:31,482 --> 00:36:33,317
And a few weeks later ,

600
00:36:33,484 --> 00:36:37,863
Giger mailed these large photographic transparencies to us at Fox .

601
00:36:38,030 --> 00:36:39,406
They came through customs -

602
00:36:39,573 --> 00:36:41,867
who didn 't understand what they were and were alarmed -

603
00:36:42,034 --> 00:36:45,579
and we had to personally go down to LAX and pick these things up .

604
00:36:46,288 --> 00:36:49,792
When I finally got these photographs that Giger had made

605
00:36:49,958 --> 00:36:51,752
of the designs he had done for the facehugger ,

606
00:36:51,919 --> 00:36:55,172
and I held them up to the light where I could see them ,

607
00:36:56,173 --> 00:36:59,343
I was stunned at what I saw .

608
00:36:59,802 --> 00:37:04,348
There was the lobed creature attached to the face of a person

609
00:37:04,515 --> 00:37:09,102
but instead of tentacles , there were fingers . Fingers .

610
00:37:09,269 --> 00:37:15,609
As soon as I saw those fingers , I knew that I would do whatever I had to do

611
00:37:15,776 --> 00:37:19,071
to get those fingers into the film .

612
00:37:19,238 --> 00:37:23,784
So I thought that the facehugger deserved to be given a great deal of our attention .

613
00:37:23,951 --> 00:37:26,328
I thought it was a very important element in the story

614
00:37:26,495 --> 00:37:29,373
and nobody seemed to be finding the time for it .

615
00:37:29,540 --> 00:37:31,959
Giger 's energies at that point

616
00:37:32,125 --> 00:37:35,128
were going into sculpting the full-sized alien -

617
00:37:35,295 --> 00:37:38,632
the life-sized one , the one which was man-shaped -

618
00:37:38,799 --> 00:37:43,053
and the facehugger wasn 't being designed . So I

619
00:37:43,470 --> 00:37:46,682
( Scott ) Oh , this is an additional scene we didn 't have in the film .

620
00:37:46,849 --> 00:37:49,393
This is where Veronica whacked you .

621
00:37:49,560 --> 00:37:51,019
- ( Weaver ) She did . - ( Scott ) Gave you this huge whack .

622
00:37:51,186 --> 00:37:53,897
- ( Weaver ) You asked her to do that ? - ( Scott ) No , absolutely not .

623
00:37:54,064 --> 00:37:57,067
- But I ... - ( Weaver ) Ha , ha , ha . Sadist .

624
00:37:57,234 --> 00:38:00,362
No , she really hit me . You can see how surprised I was .

625
00:38:00,529 --> 00:38:03,073
- And you put it in ? - ( Scott ) Well , you know ...

626
00:38:03,240 --> 00:38:06,451
By the time this is mixed and everything , I think it 's okay .

627
00:38:06,618 --> 00:38:10,581
The only reason some of these things come out - wham - has to be ...

628
00:38:10,747 --> 00:38:12,249
She really hit you . Wow , did you ...

629
00:38:12,416 --> 00:38:14,376
( Weaver ) And no one told me she was gonna do that .

630
00:38:14,543 --> 00:38:16,503
( Scott ) I ... Oop . Sorry . Ha , ha .

631
00:38:16,837 --> 00:38:22,134
( O 'Bannon ) I asked Ridley " What do you want in the facehugger ? "

632
00:38:22,301 --> 00:38:25,429
And Ridley opened up Giger 's art book

633
00:38:25,596 --> 00:38:29,850
and he found a page and he pointed to a rather amorphous painting

634
00:38:30,017 --> 00:38:34,563
which showed a kind of an organic shape with a kind of pearly texture to it .

635
00:38:34,730 --> 00:38:36,732
And he said " Something like that . "

636
00:38:36,899 --> 00:38:39,526
I said " Okay . " I said " If it looks like that , you 'll be happy ? "

637
00:38:39,693 --> 00:38:40,736
He said " Yeah . "

638
00:38:40,903 --> 00:38:45,741
So I personally designed the facehugger .

639
00:38:45,908 --> 00:38:47,409
I went over to the Art Department -

640
00:38:47,576 --> 00:38:52,039
where Ron Cobb and everybody else were slaving on designing everything -

641
00:38:52,205 --> 00:38:55,709
and I set myself up at a drawing board ,

642
00:38:55,876 --> 00:38:59,630
I put a great big piece of paper down , and the first thing I did

643
00:38:59,796 --> 00:39:05,177
was I drew a front and side outline of a human head - to start with -

644
00:39:05,344 --> 00:39:07,763
and then I began to draw the alien over that .

645
00:39:07,930 --> 00:39:09,389
I drew it side view ,

646
00:39:09,932 --> 00:39:11,600
back view and inside view .

647
00:39:11,767 --> 00:39:18,273
And first I drew a basic lobe shape to fit where it had to fit , over the face ,

648
00:39:18,440 --> 00:39:20,400
then I took the paintings -

649
00:39:20,567 --> 00:39:24,529
the very first paintings that Giger had done a year before ,

650
00:39:24,696 --> 00:39:27,115
which was the facehugger with those fingers -

651
00:39:27,282 --> 00:39:33,914
and with the greatest of care , I drew fingers onto the thing , copying his exactly .

652
00:39:34,081 --> 00:39:36,708
I mean , I had artistic training , so I could do this .

653
00:39:36,875 --> 00:39:41,088
I could copy carefully . So I got those fingers in .

654
00:39:41,254 --> 00:39:42,464
Then I went to the inner view -

655
00:39:42,631 --> 00:39:45,425
the part of the creature which would be pressed to the face -

656
00:39:45,592 --> 00:39:50,555
and I took the pearly-looking organic shape that Ridley had liked

657
00:39:50,722 --> 00:39:56,770
and I very carefully sketched the soft underbelly of the alien

658
00:39:56,937 --> 00:39:59,356
so that Ridley had what he wanted .

659
00:39:59,523 --> 00:40:06,154
And then I looked at it , and the body of the thing still needed something .

660
00:40:06,321 --> 00:40:09,950
And the point at which the fingers attached to the body of the thing ,

661
00:40:10,117 --> 00:40:11,910
I wasn 't satisfied with .

662
00:40:12,077 --> 00:40:14,454
So I turned to Ron Cobb - who was right there - I said " Ron . "

663
00:40:14,621 --> 00:40:17,249
I said " Can you help me out here ? Just take a look . "

664
00:40:17,416 --> 00:40:22,546
" See how these fingers attach to the sides of the body of the thing ? "

665
00:40:22,713 --> 00:40:25,340
I said " These fingers would continue into the body . "

666
00:40:25,507 --> 00:40:28,468
" There would be some kind of skeletal understructure under the skin . "

667
00:40:28,635 --> 00:40:33,140
" Would you please sketch in how these fingers would connect

668
00:40:33,306 --> 00:40:37,728
to the skeletal understructure as it disappears into the body of the thing ? "

669
00:40:37,894 --> 00:40:40,313
Cos Cobb is a genius at that kind of thing .

670
00:40:40,480 --> 00:40:44,317
And he took a pencil and he looked at it for a minute , and he said " Well , let 's see . "

671
00:40:44,484 --> 00:40:49,448
" If those fingers attached here , then they would probably continue on into the body . "

672
00:40:49,614 --> 00:40:52,492
" There would be a skeletal understructure like this . "

673
00:40:52,659 --> 00:40:54,494
And over the space of a few minutes ,

674
00:40:54,661 --> 00:40:57,831
he then sketched the internal skeleton of the thing ,

675
00:40:57,998 --> 00:40:59,875
and it worked . It looked right .

676
00:41:00,042 --> 00:41:02,586
I said " Okay , that now looks plausible .

677
00:41:02,753 --> 00:41:07,382
The fingers have a biologically realistic basis in the way they fit into the thing . "

678
00:41:07,549 --> 00:41:09,468
And so then I finished the drawing .

679
00:41:09,634 --> 00:41:12,179
I rendered it very , very carefully .

680
00:41:12,345 --> 00:41:16,183
And I detached the thing from the drawing table

681
00:41:16,349 --> 00:41:21,354
and I went walking over to find Ridley - I finally found Ridley -

682
00:41:21,521 --> 00:41:24,191
and I held the drawing up in front of him , I said :

683
00:41:24,357 --> 00:41:29,029
" Okay ? Facehugger ? " And he looked at it and he said " Yes , good . That 's fine . "

684
00:41:29,196 --> 00:41:31,698
I said " This is good ? " He said " Yeah . "

685
00:41:31,865 --> 00:41:38,830
I said " Good , " and I took this thing and I delivered it over to the sculptors .

686
00:41:38,997 --> 00:41:43,335
I said " Here is the design for the facehugger . Do it like this . "

687
00:41:43,502 --> 00:41:45,462
And a few days down the line ,

688
00:41:45,629 --> 00:41:49,216
they came up with a sculpture of the thing , sculpted in clay .

689
00:41:49,382 --> 00:41:55,180
They then took a cast of that and they made a mold of it in foam rubber .

690
00:41:55,347 --> 00:41:58,642
And it hasn 't been painted yet . It was simply the color of the foam rubber ,

691
00:41:58,809 --> 00:42:01,603
which was kind of the color of a manila envelope

692
00:42:01,770 --> 00:42:04,898
which was close to the color of human skin . And I looked at it .

693
00:42:05,065 --> 00:42:08,693
And up to that point I had been thinking of the alien

694
00:42:08,860 --> 00:42:14,324
as having a sort of a lizard-like color , a dark-greenish quality .

695
00:42:14,491 --> 00:42:18,703
But I looked at this facehugger there and it was the color of human skin

696
00:42:18,870 --> 00:42:20,163
and I said " You know what ? "

697
00:42:20,330 --> 00:42:25,168
I said " I have never seen a space alien which is the color of human skin . "

698
00:42:25,335 --> 00:42:31,591
I said " Doing this is not only novel , it gives it added plausibility . "

699
00:42:31,758 --> 00:42:34,553
And I said that to Ridley when he looked at the thing .

700
00:42:34,719 --> 00:42:37,430
By then , they had inserted a wire armature inside the thing

701
00:42:37,597 --> 00:42:40,767
so that you could stand it up and wrap its fingers around something .

702
00:42:40,934 --> 00:42:44,062
And there it was , the facehugger , the color of human skin .

703
00:42:44,229 --> 00:42:45,939
I said " Why don 't we not paint it ? "

704
00:42:46,106 --> 00:42:49,276
I said " Why don 't we leave it like that ? Skin color . "

705
00:42:49,442 --> 00:42:51,945
And again Ridley liked it and he went for it .

706
00:42:57,617 --> 00:42:58,952
( Scott ) I just went for the script .

707
00:42:59,119 --> 00:43:02,455
The script appealed to me in all the elements and graphic design .

708
00:43:02,622 --> 00:43:07,794
And the sets started to come together in my head because I 'm a designer anyway ,

709
00:43:07,961 --> 00:43:10,088
so I was able to storyboard the whole movie .

710
00:43:10,255 --> 00:43:14,092
And at that moment the movie was 4.6 .

711
00:43:14,843 --> 00:43:17,304
I went back to London having said " I 'll do the film , "

712
00:43:17,470 --> 00:43:19,806
and I storyboarded extensively .

713
00:43:19,973 --> 00:43:21,892
And by doing that , you know , you 're working ...

714
00:43:22,058 --> 00:43:25,270
I find storyboarding 's interesting because it actually helps you think on paper .

715
00:43:26,771 --> 00:43:28,773
It makes you really pin down in your head exactly what you 're gonna do .

716
00:43:28,940 --> 00:43:30,775
You start to see the scene .

717
00:43:30,942 --> 00:43:33,403
From that , we kind of doubled the budget .

718
00:43:33,570 --> 00:43:36,448
Which just shows the power of the old storyboard

719
00:43:36,615 --> 00:43:38,491
because they suddenly start to see things ...

720
00:43:38,658 --> 00:43:45,957
There was a lot more here than , I think , just six people in the old , dark house .

721
00:43:52,964 --> 00:43:56,718
I always liked the wardrobe in the film . I thought it was really well done .

722
00:43:56,885 --> 00:44:01,640
And Giger designed the spacesuits .

723
00:44:01,973 --> 00:44:05,101
To a large extent he did that ,

724
00:44:05,268 --> 00:44:09,314
and then it was carried out by John Mollo .

725
00:44:09,814 --> 00:44:12,943
Then John did also all the other ... This ...

726
00:44:13,109 --> 00:44:16,071
The pale blue and the badges and the ...

727
00:44:16,238 --> 00:44:19,366
The badges were designed and the ...

728
00:44:19,532 --> 00:44:23,870
You know , the broken-down , slightly worn-out view of the uniforms .

729
00:44:24,037 --> 00:44:27,707
And I thought it was pretty good , kind of layered and interesting .

730
00:44:28,333 --> 00:44:31,378
Found a real flight suit for Sigourney , so I couldn 't do any better than that

731
00:44:31,544 --> 00:44:36,716
because those flight suits in those days were kind of new , from high-flying jet pilots .

732
00:44:36,883 --> 00:44:38,385
I always thought " Wow , they 're great . "

733
00:44:38,551 --> 00:44:41,972
I didn 't quite know what all the laces did but they were kind of sexy ,

734
00:44:42,138 --> 00:44:44,683
so that 's what she got .

735
00:44:45,892 --> 00:44:48,019
But John designed all the badges and the prints .

736
00:44:48,186 --> 00:44:52,565
The patches were all printed . Not printed actually , they 're stitched .

737
00:44:55,610 --> 00:44:58,738
I wish I 'd kept some of these things but I didn 't .

738
00:44:59,239 --> 00:45:01,574
One never does at the time .

739
00:45:02,117 --> 00:45:07,080
The symbol above the monitor at the back , which are the wings ,

740
00:45:07,247 --> 00:45:10,583
is actually taken from an Egyptian temple .

741
00:45:10,750 --> 00:45:13,420
And a lot of the elements , architecture in here -

742
00:45:13,586 --> 00:45:16,464
if you look around - are rather Egyptian .

743
00:45:18,008 --> 00:45:20,260
The shot of somebody in a room ,

744
00:45:20,427 --> 00:45:24,597
or where you get a shot on their back , is vulnerable ,

745
00:45:24,764 --> 00:45:26,683
so people get very tense .

746
00:45:26,850 --> 00:45:30,020
You could start to acknowledge the signals and symbols

747
00:45:30,186 --> 00:45:33,606
which are almost like ... Maybe it 's something primordial

748
00:45:33,773 --> 00:45:36,359
because we were all fundamentally born as hunters ,

749
00:45:36,526 --> 00:45:40,322
and therefore there 's those little elements

750
00:45:40,613 --> 00:45:47,787
of the basics , that you can start to play with and make people paranoid , uneasy , fearful .

751
00:45:48,455 --> 00:45:50,457
I think it 's getting progressively more hard to do ,

752
00:45:50,623 --> 00:45:55,211
mainly because there 's such an abundance of hard-core thrillers

753
00:45:55,378 --> 00:46:00,008
and every conceivable kind of creature , and it 's starting to get used up

754
00:46:00,175 --> 00:46:02,802
until somebody comes along with something unique .

755
00:46:02,969 --> 00:46:05,597
I haven 't seen anything unique for a while .

756
00:46:05,764 --> 00:46:08,516
It tends to be more of the same .

757
00:46:08,683 --> 00:46:11,561
I think that 's probably why I haven 't tried to do another science fiction

758
00:46:11,728 --> 00:46:12,771
since Blade Runner .

759
00:46:12,937 --> 00:46:17,192
Because I haven 't really come across anything that really had the story .

760
00:46:17,359 --> 00:46:19,277
It 's all about the story and then the characters .

761
00:46:19,444 --> 00:46:21,988
If you 've got the story , the chances are you got the characters .

762
00:46:22,155 --> 00:46:24,949
Then what happens after that , the art direction ,

763
00:46:25,116 --> 00:46:29,662
it becomes relatively easy with the right people .

764
00:46:31,289 --> 00:46:33,041
( Shusett ) And you can see how seriously Ian Holm -

765
00:46:33,208 --> 00:46:37,670
a great Shakespearean actor already , admired for his London stage work -

766
00:46:37,837 --> 00:46:42,884
you can see how into it and how seriously they took not doing this like camp .

767
00:46:43,051 --> 00:46:46,429
And I 'll tell you , that happened within the first few weeks .

768
00:46:47,055 --> 00:46:50,683
And the script was so good , I say in all native arrogance .

769
00:46:50,850 --> 00:46:53,520
The actors were so into it cos they could see by the dailies ...

770
00:46:53,686 --> 00:46:56,815
We were all looking at this stuff and it was working so great .

771
00:46:56,981 --> 00:46:59,484
If they ever thought " Oh , I 'm doing this as a paying job . "

772
00:46:59,651 --> 00:47:02,570
" This is gonna be stupid , but Ridley 's so brilliant it 'll look good ,

773
00:47:02,737 --> 00:47:04,989
and it 'll be kind of dumb or a modest little thing . "

774
00:47:05,156 --> 00:47:09,577
" And they 'll say a little sleeper , but silly . " I 'd say , by the third week ,

775
00:47:09,744 --> 00:47:15,041
all of us , all felt this would be an all-time great , classic film .

776
00:47:19,754 --> 00:47:21,673
( Scott ) We made up this set because I said :

777
00:47:21,840 --> 00:47:24,551
" The captain must wanna go somewhere just to get away from everyone . "

778
00:47:24,717 --> 00:47:27,887
So I figured the logic would be he goes and hides in one of the shuttles

779
00:47:28,972 --> 00:47:31,057
just to get away from everyone ,

780
00:47:31,224 --> 00:47:35,311
and play something very normal . Like that was , I think , Mozart .

781
00:47:37,063 --> 00:47:40,733
I always thought this was really a beautiful , very successful set

782
00:47:40,900 --> 00:47:43,027
that we always wanted to be circular .

783
00:47:43,194 --> 00:47:47,866
They always cost more money because everything circular is more expensive .

784
00:47:48,825 --> 00:47:51,077
But it paid off . I think it was absolutely great .

785
00:47:51,244 --> 00:47:56,499
And it 's almost like it 's positioned beneath ... The infirmary is beneath the kitchen .

786
00:47:56,666 --> 00:48:00,753
This will be a multi-deck ship , probably four or five decks .

787
00:48:01,337 --> 00:48:04,757
It 's like , if you 're trying to lay a floor in your house and you got a circular room ,

788
00:48:04,924 --> 00:48:08,595
your use of tile is gonna be more expensive than straight edges .

789
00:48:08,761 --> 00:48:11,806
So if you apply that to a big set , you got a similar thing .

790
00:48:11,973 --> 00:48:14,350
You got a high percentage of waste .

791
00:48:16,811 --> 00:48:19,522
Halfway through the film you 're starting to get a bit weary of :

792
00:48:19,689 --> 00:48:21,149
" Oh , God , another scary scene . "

793
00:48:21,316 --> 00:48:24,777
" How do we deal with this ? Because nothing really happens . "

794
00:48:24,944 --> 00:48:29,032
So now you resort to one or two tricks .

795
00:48:29,199 --> 00:48:31,993
And when I talked about going on people 's back view

796
00:48:32,160 --> 00:48:34,746
or putting the camera in a funny corner position ,

797
00:48:34,913 --> 00:48:38,208
where you began to wonder " Is that the alien ? "

798
00:48:38,374 --> 00:48:40,710
" What has happened to it ? Where has it gone ? "

799
00:48:40,877 --> 00:48:43,546
You know what it looks like , but you don 't know where it is .

800
00:48:44,130 --> 00:48:47,675
That 's a cheap gag , but it makes everyone leap out of their skin .

801
00:48:48,718 --> 00:48:51,596
You know , sometimes you keep these things going long enough ,

802
00:48:51,763 --> 00:48:55,767
you get the tension really builds up with very little .

803
00:48:56,226 --> 00:49:00,104
Again , playing the silence is interesting

804
00:49:00,271 --> 00:49:04,901
because silence , oddly enough , tells you something 's gonna happen .

805
00:49:05,068 --> 00:49:07,737
You don 't know who 's gonna get it ,

806
00:49:07,904 --> 00:49:11,449
but I think at this moment you think " This has gone on too long . "

807
00:49:11,616 --> 00:49:14,869
" It 's too quiet . Is it him ? "

808
00:49:17,163 --> 00:49:20,959
( Skerritt ) It 's one of the first times I sat through a picture I was involved with

809
00:49:21,125 --> 00:49:23,211
where it scared the hell out of me , so , I mean ...

810
00:49:23,378 --> 00:49:27,257
You know , I saw all this stuff going on when we were shooting it ,

811
00:49:27,423 --> 00:49:31,511
but the magic of how Ridley shoots and the timing of this .

812
00:49:31,678 --> 00:49:38,935
You know ? It 's the slow ... And you take the time to get every little slow move in .

813
00:49:40,353 --> 00:49:44,023
( Scott ) When you see this out of context and are able to examine it ...

814
00:49:44,190 --> 00:49:46,734
That always gave everyone a big bump .

815
00:49:46,901 --> 00:49:50,321
And funny enough , I was always uncomfortable about that .

816
00:49:53,116 --> 00:49:55,493
But I think by now , we had ' em .

817
00:49:56,452 --> 00:49:57,954
I always remember the producer saying :

818
00:49:58,121 --> 00:50:01,749
" Don 't worry , you 've got ' em . You 've got ' em now . "

819
00:50:06,296 --> 00:50:12,802
Here we have the visit from the fishmongers where we use the real thing .

820
00:50:12,969 --> 00:50:18,808
Basically , carefully positioned with tweezers - that 's a big oyster -

821
00:50:18,975 --> 00:50:23,479
into a case , which is actually made of plastic .

822
00:50:25,982 --> 00:50:29,569
( O 'Bannon ) There you go . See , he 's just lifting up the edge of the oyster .

823
00:50:29,736 --> 00:50:31,571
These were the tricks that we used

824
00:50:31,738 --> 00:50:35,575
in our efforts to come up with a space creature

825
00:50:35,742 --> 00:50:40,830
which , on the one hand , looked completely different than anything you 'd seen before ,

826
00:50:40,997 --> 00:50:43,166
but on the other hand ,

827
00:50:43,333 --> 00:50:46,794
looked absolutely like something that was alive and made of real flesh .

828
00:50:46,961 --> 00:50:50,423
And on the third hand , is terrifying to look at .

829
00:50:50,590 --> 00:50:55,845
And I believe that we succeeded and I am pleased with that .

830
00:50:56,012 --> 00:50:58,931
You see ? There 's the value of novelty .

831
00:50:59,390 --> 00:51:05,104
If it 's new and you haven 't seen it before , it has impact .

832
00:51:05,396 --> 00:51:08,816
Once something becomes familiar ... No matter how well it 's done ,

833
00:51:08,983 --> 00:51:10,026
if it 's familiar ...

834
00:51:10,193 --> 00:51:13,905
Well , you know the old saying , familiarity breeds contempt .

835
00:51:14,072 --> 00:51:16,240
And it 's a shame in a way , because you see Alien now

836
00:51:16,407 --> 00:51:18,868
and Alien no longer has novelty .

837
00:51:19,035 --> 00:51:22,330
So that Alien seen today by a contemporary audience

838
00:51:22,497 --> 00:51:26,584
has only a fraction of the impact it had when the picture first came out

839
00:51:26,751 --> 00:51:29,045
because they 've seen it all before .

840
00:51:29,212 --> 00:51:36,094
But at the time , there was a lot in Alien that was entirely new to an audience

841
00:51:36,260 --> 00:51:38,554
and the impact that it had was considerable .

842
00:51:39,138 --> 00:51:42,684
And it was even received well by the critical community .

843
00:51:42,850 --> 00:51:45,853
Cos in those days , science-fiction films were very much looked down upon

844
00:51:46,020 --> 00:51:48,815
by movie critics , and they liked Alien .

845
00:51:48,981 --> 00:51:51,442
( Scott ) I mean , really , this is the only horror film -

846
00:51:51,609 --> 00:51:53,986
if you could call it that - I 've done .

847
00:51:54,153 --> 00:51:57,532
The thing that really , at that moment , nailed me to the wall

848
00:51:57,699 --> 00:51:59,701
was Texas Chain Saw Massacre .

849
00:51:59,867 --> 00:52:02,787
I haven 't seen that in recent years so I don 't know how it would play -

850
00:52:02,954 --> 00:52:04,914
whether it is what it is or whether it stands up -

851
00:52:05,081 --> 00:52:07,375
but at the time it was so shocking .

852
00:52:07,542 --> 00:52:10,128
They went to places that no one had actually gone .

853
00:52:10,294 --> 00:52:12,839
And you think " My God , he 's not gonna do that , " and then he does .

854
00:52:13,005 --> 00:52:20,304
But I would say that probably the best and still the most intelligent all-around ,

855
00:52:20,471 --> 00:52:21,973
is probably The Exorcist , you know .

856
00:52:22,140 --> 00:52:29,063
The first Exorcist was really good with a great , very simple narrative idea .

857
00:52:29,272 --> 00:52:34,110
William Friedkin made it all work , cos to even make a film

858
00:52:34,277 --> 00:52:38,740
which begins with a film within a film , is very dangerous and tenuous .

859
00:52:38,906 --> 00:52:43,161
But he made that work . He made the Irish director work ,

860
00:52:43,327 --> 00:52:50,042
and then he made the star living in her house in Georgetown , work .

861
00:52:50,209 --> 00:52:56,048
And he took almost the unlikely by making it real , make it stick .

862
00:52:56,215 --> 00:52:59,135
I think that all in all , it 's still one of the best .

863
00:52:59,302 --> 00:53:02,764
There are many versions of that . That 's the problem ,

864
00:53:02,930 --> 00:53:05,308
people settle for less .

865
00:53:05,516 --> 00:53:08,186
But then we have to make films .

866
00:53:08,561 --> 00:53:12,690
( O 'Bannon ) I was trying to figure out who these people were in the spaceship .

867
00:53:12,857 --> 00:53:16,819
What was the spaceship doing out there and who were the people on board ?

868
00:53:16,986 --> 00:53:22,366
So I was looking for something different in order to break the audience 's mental mold .

869
00:53:22,533 --> 00:53:24,827
I was doing everything I could

870
00:53:24,994 --> 00:53:30,583
to forbid the audience to think of this as just a space movie .

871
00:53:30,750 --> 00:53:33,419
Because I wanted to isolate them .

872
00:53:33,586 --> 00:53:35,880
I wanted this terrible sense of isolation

873
00:53:36,047 --> 00:53:40,426
so you just knew that you weren 't gonna get help from home at all .

874
00:53:41,761 --> 00:53:42,887
( Stanton ) And I had this line ...

875
00:53:43,054 --> 00:53:45,848
You remember I had the line ... " Every time somebody would say something

876
00:53:46,015 --> 00:53:47,558
you say ' right . " ' " Right . "

877
00:53:47,725 --> 00:53:51,270
Well , every ... When Yaphet would say it .

878
00:53:51,437 --> 00:53:57,527
So we 're doing a scene , one scene where Sigourney says to me :

879
00:53:57,693 --> 00:54:02,156
" Brett , why do you always say ' right' every time he says something ? "

880
00:54:02,323 --> 00:54:03,783
In the middle of the take , you know ?

881
00:54:03,950 --> 00:54:05,701
And I said " Why don 't you go fuck yourself ? "

882
00:54:05,868 --> 00:54:07,286
( Stanton laughing )

883
00:54:07,453 --> 00:54:10,748
And they said " cut " and ...

884
00:54:11,332 --> 00:54:14,877
And that wasn 't in the script , of course .

885
00:54:15,336 --> 00:54:18,965
And Tom Skerritt or Yaphet or one of them said :

886
00:54:19,632 --> 00:54:21,926
" Well , if he 's gonna change the lines ... "

887
00:54:22,093 --> 00:54:23,135
( Stanton laughing )

888
00:54:23,302 --> 00:54:25,930
It created a big furor .

889
00:54:31,561 --> 00:54:35,439
( Scott ) I think there 's an uncomfortable atmosphere to the scene

890
00:54:35,606 --> 00:54:40,319
where they can 't believe it 's that simple , basically .

891
00:54:41,904 --> 00:54:46,868
When you see the film with a proper mix and big sound ,

892
00:54:47,034 --> 00:54:51,747
you can hear a lot of very subtle sounds all the time . They 're always there .

893
00:54:51,914 --> 00:54:53,749
So they 're working on your paranoia .

894
00:54:53,916 --> 00:54:57,503
And I think these atmospherics are terribly important .

895
00:54:58,504 --> 00:55:04,802
This is not scored , this is just uneasy , almost organic sounds

896
00:55:04,969 --> 00:55:07,597
that make you feel uncomfortable .

897
00:55:08,097 --> 00:55:13,603
And you don 't know what it is . Is it a system of air conditioning ?

898
00:55:13,769 --> 00:55:15,187
Is it the sound of the ship ?

899
00:55:15,354 --> 00:55:19,817
So basically Jimmy was asked to design the whole idea of the sound of the ship ,

900
00:55:19,984 --> 00:55:23,112
other than just the usual big rumble .

901
00:55:23,279 --> 00:55:26,240
But the big rumble is almost there throughout everything .

902
00:55:26,407 --> 00:55:33,664
So here we have this jocularity , and Ash 's slight watchfulness .

903
00:55:34,790 --> 00:55:36,959
( Rawlings ) Yeah , the whole point of this is to play this

904
00:55:37,126 --> 00:55:39,295
as if nothing was gonna happen at all .

905
00:55:40,046 --> 00:55:44,550
But I don 't think anybody realized what was gonna happen , even the actors .

906
00:55:44,717 --> 00:55:46,427
I 'm sure they didn 't .

907
00:55:46,594 --> 00:55:49,138
I mean , they knew what was about to happen ,

908
00:55:49,305 --> 00:55:52,725
but the way it happened was a shock to them

909
00:55:52,892 --> 00:55:55,686
as well as the audience .

910
00:55:55,853 --> 00:55:57,730
Especially her .

911
00:55:58,648 --> 00:56:03,235
( Skerritt ) I remember coming in and watching Ridley set this thing up with John

912
00:56:03,402 --> 00:56:05,529
so I kind of had some idea of what the mechanics were .

913
00:56:05,696 --> 00:56:08,366
I don 't think anybody else had ... Any of you guys had gone in there .

914
00:56:08,532 --> 00:56:09,575
( Cartwright ) No , they wouldn 't let us .

915
00:56:09,742 --> 00:56:11,953
They kept us upstairs in those dressing rooms for hours .

916
00:56:12,119 --> 00:56:13,955
( Skerritt ) Well , I was down and watching it and ...

917
00:56:14,121 --> 00:56:17,625
The look you get on Veronica 's face is the real thing .

918
00:56:17,792 --> 00:56:20,127
She had no idea what was gonna happen .

919
00:56:20,294 --> 00:56:26,634
Ridley used some rather uncomplimentary intestines of animals , I think .

920
00:56:27,218 --> 00:56:29,762
( Cartwright ) Well , there were huge buckets of offal around and ...

921
00:56:29,929 --> 00:56:30,972
( Skerritt ) Yeah .

922
00:56:31,138 --> 00:56:32,515
( Cartwright ) But what was there , three cameras ?

923
00:56:32,682 --> 00:56:34,600
- Three or four cameras . - ( Skerritt ) Yeah .

924
00:56:34,767 --> 00:56:38,312
- This is some serious indigestion going on . - ( Cartwright ) Then they rigged him .

925
00:56:38,479 --> 00:56:41,023
- ( Stanton ) Yeah , the airplane food . - ( Skerritt ) Yeah . Heh , heh .

926
00:56:41,190 --> 00:56:45,027
( Cartwright ) I remember they had the shirt slightly slit .

927
00:56:45,194 --> 00:56:46,278
( Stanton ) Yeah .

928
00:56:46,445 --> 00:56:49,865
( Cartwright ) And then they stopped it because it wasn 't cut enough .

929
00:56:50,032 --> 00:56:52,034
And they told me I 'd get a little blood on my face

930
00:56:52,201 --> 00:56:54,036
and they had an entire jet pointed at it .

931
00:56:54,203 --> 00:56:57,832
( Stanton ) That was the strongest vision I had in the movie ,

932
00:56:57,999 --> 00:56:59,792
when I looked up and saw blood on her face .

933
00:56:59,959 --> 00:57:02,336
( Skerritt ) Yeah , yeah . They were out to get her .

934
00:57:02,503 --> 00:57:05,047
- ( Stanton ) And I thought it was for real . - ( Skerritt ) They were out to get Veronica .

935
00:57:05,214 --> 00:57:08,259
Look at this . Look at the look on Veronica 's face .

936
00:57:08,426 --> 00:57:11,721
I love this . She was saying " What am I doing here ? "

937
00:57:11,887 --> 00:57:15,391
( Stanton ) That ... Yeah , right in there . That 's , whoa .

938
00:57:15,891 --> 00:57:17,643
Whoa .

939
00:57:19,103 --> 00:57:21,397
( Cartwright ) It 's those little teeth with the little projectile .

940
00:57:21,564 --> 00:57:22,857
( Skerritt ) Oh , the smile .

941
00:57:24,984 --> 00:57:28,029
" I 'm a scientist . Don 't touch it . "

942
00:57:28,904 --> 00:57:29,905
( Stanton ) That Ian .

943
00:57:32,742 --> 00:57:35,244
( Cartwright ) Remember , it was the guy on a skateboard underneath that table ,

944
00:57:35,411 --> 00:57:36,954
- ( Skerritt ) Yeah . - ( Cartwright ) with a cut in the table ,

945
00:57:37,121 --> 00:57:40,791
and then they had him on a dolly and they whipped him out .

946
00:57:40,958 --> 00:57:42,418
( Skerritt ) Yeah . Yeah .

947
00:57:42,585 --> 00:57:47,882
( Cartwright ) It was , like , simple stuff but it just looked so unbelievable .

948
00:57:48,257 --> 00:57:51,052
( Stanton ) How many times did we do that ? Two or three times ?

949
00:57:51,218 --> 00:57:53,345
- ( Cartwright ) Well , no , one time . - ( Skerritt ) No , I think it was one time .

950
00:57:53,512 --> 00:57:55,639
( Cartwright ) It was a one-off deal . They started to do it ,

951
00:57:55,806 --> 00:57:58,976
they realized the shirt wasn 't cut enough so they needed to go back and do that ,

952
00:57:59,143 --> 00:58:02,646
and then that was a one time . And like four cameras .

953
00:58:02,813 --> 00:58:06,692
And when I got hit in that face , remember , I backed up ...

954
00:58:06,859 --> 00:58:09,653
( Stanton ) Yeah , that 's my strongest vision in the movie , of blood on your face .

955
00:58:09,820 --> 00:58:12,948
( Cartwright ) And my knees hit the back of that banquette and I flipped upside down ,

956
00:58:13,115 --> 00:58:14,992
and my boots were sticking up in the air .

957
00:58:15,159 --> 00:58:17,161
( Skerritt ) You were immobilized for four days .

958
00:58:17,328 --> 00:58:19,747
( Stanton ) And I thought it was real .

959
00:58:19,914 --> 00:58:22,083
( commentators laughing )

960
00:58:38,849 --> 00:58:43,938
( Scott ) Again , Jerry 's theme , which tells us there 's more .

961
00:58:44,105 --> 00:58:46,148
It 's threatening .

962
00:58:46,482 --> 00:58:52,321
And somehow chilling . Chilly , beautiful and elegant .

963
00:58:58,285 --> 00:59:01,997
I think O 'Bannon and ... I know Ronnie adored it .

964
00:59:02,581 --> 00:59:06,335
I 'm not sure about Dan . I 'd always wanted Dan 's approval .

965
00:59:06,502 --> 00:59:09,421
- I thought Dark Star was really amazing . - ( Weaver ) Yeah .

966
00:59:09,588 --> 00:59:12,591
- ( Scott ) Very humorous piece . Brilliant . - ( Weaver ) Yeah .

967
00:59:13,425 --> 00:59:15,052
( Scott ) And that was directed by John Carpenter .

968
00:59:16,887 --> 00:59:18,639
Dan had written that one .

969
00:59:18,806 --> 00:59:22,560
Dan had got involved in some of the directing process .

970
00:59:22,726 --> 00:59:25,563
I know Dan 's deepest , innermost dream

971
00:59:25,729 --> 00:59:28,607
would have been to have directed this movie , right ?

972
00:59:28,774 --> 00:59:33,028
And therefore I felt , A , the fact that he was relieved ...

973
00:59:33,195 --> 00:59:35,489
Because oddly enough he really loved The Duellists ,

974
00:59:35,656 --> 00:59:38,284
which is really strange because he 's a science-fiction fanatic

975
00:59:38,450 --> 00:59:40,661
and I wasn 't .

976
00:59:41,078 --> 00:59:43,831
So whoever chose me to do this is bizarre .

977
00:59:43,998 --> 00:59:45,499
( Weaver ) You chose yourself .

978
00:59:45,666 --> 00:59:48,711
( Scott ) Well , I just read it . Yeah , but I was fifth in line .

979
00:59:48,878 --> 00:59:51,005
I was the fifth or sixth director .

980
00:59:51,172 --> 00:59:52,756
( Weaver ) You mean other people had turned it down ?

981
00:59:52,923 --> 00:59:54,967
( Scott ) Oh , yeah . You want me to name them ?

982
00:59:55,134 --> 00:59:56,844
( Weaver ) Well , that 's because ... Ha , ha , ha .

983
00:59:57,011 --> 00:59:59,805
( Scott ) I know who they were . I was thinking " Wow , what a dummy . "

984
00:59:59,972 --> 01:00:04,518
( Weaver ) But also I remember being a dummy too because when I read it -

985
01:00:04,685 --> 01:00:07,563
not knowing what the alien looked like -

986
01:00:07,730 --> 01:00:11,108
I just saw this big , like , gummy bear , you know ?

987
01:00:11,275 --> 01:00:13,569
- ( Scott ) That would have been awful . - ( Weaver ) I know , it would have been .

988
01:00:13,736 --> 01:00:17,114
That 's why when I first met you and you said " What do you think of the script ? "

989
01:00:17,281 --> 01:00:19,783
I said " Well , I think it 's very bleak . "

990
01:00:19,950 --> 01:00:24,371
And I remember the casting person , Goldberg , kind of going ...

991
01:00:24,538 --> 01:00:26,248
You know , like , " Shut up , stupid . "

992
01:00:27,124 --> 01:00:28,375
( Weaver laughing )

993
01:00:28,542 --> 01:00:30,628
" You 're lucky just to be here . "

994
01:00:30,794 --> 01:00:32,963
( Scott ) But " bleak " is right because that 's what we wanted .

995
01:00:33,130 --> 01:00:35,299
- ( Weaver ) Well , and once I saw the designs , - ( Scott ) Yeah .

996
01:00:35,466 --> 01:00:39,386
( Weaver ) it completely changed the way I thought about the script .

997
01:00:40,971 --> 01:00:44,767
( O 'Bannon ) I was , of course , tempted to develop these characters up and give them ,

998
01:00:44,934 --> 01:00:51,649
you know , full psychological profiles and , you know , personal problems ,

999
01:00:51,815 --> 01:00:54,068
and the whole panoply of character stuff

1000
01:00:54,235 --> 01:00:59,949
that is always done by knee-jerk reaction in every movie and every screenplay .

1001
01:01:00,115 --> 01:01:04,620
Except for one thing , and that was that it bored me .

1002
01:01:04,787 --> 01:01:06,413
I didn 't give a rat 's ass .

1003
01:01:06,580 --> 01:01:11,752
I didn 't care about their personal psychological quirks .

1004
01:01:11,919 --> 01:01:17,258
Except insomuch as it had an immediate bearing on the situation at hand .

1005
01:01:17,424 --> 01:01:25,015
I didn 't want to stop and tell the life stories of these characters because I didn 't care .

1006
01:01:25,182 --> 01:01:28,143
I cared about the monster that was going to kill them .

1007
01:01:28,310 --> 01:01:29,853
That , I cared about .

1008
01:01:30,020 --> 01:01:33,983
So I didn 't do any of that in the script . I did none of it at all .

1009
01:01:40,364 --> 01:01:45,786
( Scott ) Everybody really worked great in the process of a film that ,

1010
01:01:45,953 --> 01:01:51,000
in a funny kind of way , doesn 't call for background

1011
01:01:51,166 --> 01:01:52,501
and who 's writing to who at home ,

1012
01:01:52,668 --> 01:01:55,129
and how are you getting that information back to families and :

1013
01:01:55,296 --> 01:01:56,338
( Scott gibbering )

1014
01:01:56,505 --> 01:01:59,466
Because actually what it was , is a B movie .

1015
01:01:59,633 --> 01:02:02,094
- It 's " Seven Little ... " What 's ? - ( Weaver ) " Ten Little Indians . "

1016
01:02:02,261 --> 01:02:05,848
( Scott ) Ten little Indians in the old dark house , and that 's what it is .

1017
01:02:06,015 --> 01:02:11,020
But I think what happened is , we all elevated it .

1018
01:02:11,895 --> 01:02:15,691
( Skerritt ) Well , there was a scene here I remember , where Yaphet came in -

1019
01:02:15,858 --> 01:02:18,527
and Yaphet loves to kind of stir things up -

1020
01:02:18,694 --> 01:02:23,532
and he came in to work one afternoon - it may have been this afternoon here -

1021
01:02:23,699 --> 01:02:28,162
and the English crew was very quiet and always very gentlemanly ,

1022
01:02:28,329 --> 01:02:32,166
standing back as Yaphet came on the set and said :

1023
01:02:32,333 --> 01:02:34,418
( in gruff voice ) " Let 's cut this quiet shit . Come on . "

1024
01:02:34,585 --> 01:02:36,670
" You guys stand around , you don 't do nothing . "

1025
01:02:36,837 --> 01:02:39,548
" You just act like gentlemen . "

1026
01:02:39,715 --> 01:02:42,718
( in normal voice ) And he really got it going and finally calmed down -

1027
01:02:42,885 --> 01:02:45,971
he got his blood circulating at that point -

1028
01:02:46,138 --> 01:02:49,850
and I was standing in the back and there were a couple of Englishmen in front of me

1029
01:02:50,017 --> 01:02:51,602
and one looks at the other , he says

1030
01:02:51,769 --> 01:02:53,645
( in English accent ) " Isn 't it grand being English ? "

1031
01:02:53,812 --> 01:02:55,814
( commentators laughing )

1032
01:02:55,981 --> 01:02:58,317
( Stanton ) He 's got a point , really .

1033
01:02:58,984 --> 01:03:00,819
( Cartwright ) Oh , I think we 're on to something here .

1034
01:03:00,986 --> 01:03:03,822
- ( Skerritt in normal voice ) Ah . Jonesy , - ( Cartwright ) Oh , that 's ... Oh , it 's the cat .

1035
01:03:03,989 --> 01:03:05,741
( Skerritt ) you beast , you .

1036
01:03:06,825 --> 01:03:09,328
( Rawlings ) It was a fantastic exercise , I think , for both of us

1037
01:03:09,495 --> 01:03:13,082
because this was , I think , Ridley 's second film .

1038
01:03:13,248 --> 01:03:16,627
And it was my third , I did two others before that .

1039
01:03:17,378 --> 01:03:19,088
So it was all very new ,

1040
01:03:19,254 --> 01:03:22,800
and I don 't think I 've ever worked as hard as we did on this picture -

1041
01:03:22,966 --> 01:03:25,886
the sort of hours we put in to do it .

1042
01:03:26,053 --> 01:03:29,556
You know , we tried everything we knew how , all different ways around .

1043
01:03:29,723 --> 01:03:34,603
The whole idea was to terrorize people as much as possible .

1044
01:03:38,440 --> 01:03:42,611
And that only comes with , like , pushing people into a corner endlessly .

1045
01:03:42,778 --> 01:03:47,074
Going as far as you can go , but not going over the top .

1046
01:03:47,241 --> 01:03:49,451
( Scott ) And , yeah , I remember running the film in , I think ...

1047
01:03:49,618 --> 01:03:53,497
Where was it , in London ? Or was it L.A. , the Egyptian ?

1048
01:03:53,664 --> 01:03:56,625
But I remember running it in London - and the film in L.A. -

1049
01:03:56,792 --> 01:04:01,672
and I knew we had something really extraordinary

1050
01:04:01,839 --> 01:04:06,343
mainly because of the reaction , not just during but afterwards .

1051
01:04:06,510 --> 01:04:10,222
There was this kind of stunned silence .

1052
01:04:10,389 --> 01:04:14,476
And I remember Harry coming up to me - I think it was at the Egyptian -

1053
01:04:14,643 --> 01:04:18,981
and he 's so sweet , and Harry looked at me , he said " Thanks for the close-ups , man . "

1054
01:04:19,189 --> 01:04:21,066
- ( Weaver ) Oh , sweet . - ( Scott ) Yeah .

1055
01:04:21,233 --> 01:04:27,197
And he 'd meant when he walks through and goes " Here , kitty , kitty , kitty . "

1056
01:04:27,364 --> 01:04:28,574
- Which is great . - ( Weaver ) Yeah .

1057
01:04:28,740 --> 01:04:31,368
- ( Scott ) That moment , you know he is gone . - ( Weaver ) I know .

1058
01:04:31,535 --> 01:04:37,666
( Scott ) But he was very sweet , and he and Yaphet made this great duo .

1059
01:04:38,917 --> 01:04:44,381
In fact , I think the whole , to me , is probably the best ensemble I 've ever had .

1060
01:04:48,510 --> 01:04:51,972
( Stanton ) Now , this is where I screwed up . I could never play terror .

1061
01:04:52,139 --> 01:04:57,144
I can play crying , I can laugh , I can cry , I can do everything but playing terror .

1062
01:04:57,311 --> 01:05:01,315
And I didn 't know it at the time but I found out later how to play terror ,

1063
01:05:01,482 --> 01:05:03,442
and I didn 't use it in this part .

1064
01:05:03,609 --> 01:05:07,946
It worked , but I wish I 'd have known it .

1065
01:05:08,113 --> 01:05:12,659
You don 't look scared , you just look like " I 've never seen anything like this before . "

1066
01:05:12,826 --> 01:05:15,454
Like , that 's all you have to do .

1067
01:05:15,621 --> 01:05:17,080
( Cartwright ) Ew . There 's the skin .

1068
01:05:17,247 --> 01:05:19,917
- It 's shedding its skin . - ( Skerritt ) Yeah .

1069
01:05:20,375 --> 01:05:22,920
Something 's up , Harry .

1070
01:05:23,795 --> 01:05:26,757
( Stanton ) God , they 're sickening , the images they got in here .

1071
01:05:26,924 --> 01:05:28,300
( Cartwright chuckles )

1072
01:05:28,467 --> 01:05:30,552
Disgusting .

1073
01:05:33,805 --> 01:05:36,600
( Rawlings ) This is great sound-wise too .

1074
01:05:36,767 --> 01:05:40,354
When you just enter this , like , rain forest

1075
01:05:40,521 --> 01:05:44,149
with the rain and the dripping just surrounding you .

1076
01:05:46,318 --> 01:05:49,404
( Scott ) Again , sticking to your guns . " Why the water ? "

1077
01:05:49,571 --> 01:05:51,907
So I would just say " Why not ? " " Why the chains ? "

1078
01:05:52,074 --> 01:05:53,992
" Well , the chains aren 't very high-tech . "

1079
01:05:54,159 --> 01:05:56,828
Said " Yeah , but you know what ? You 've still gotta let things down

1080
01:05:56,995 --> 01:06:00,832
so it 's still gonna be rope or water . It 's not necessarily electronic . "

1081
01:06:00,999 --> 01:06:03,502
So I had the chains dressed cos the room looked a bit blank

1082
01:06:03,669 --> 01:06:06,255
and I needed the movement in there .

1083
01:06:06,421 --> 01:06:08,465
" How is it moving ? " I said " I don 't care . "

1084
01:06:08,632 --> 01:06:12,386
" Where 's the water coming from ? " I said " Condensation . "

1085
01:06:13,595 --> 01:06:15,681
" Why the condensation ? "

1086
01:06:15,847 --> 01:06:19,476
" Well , because something 's gone wrong on the ship , but they can ... "

1087
01:06:19,643 --> 01:06:23,647
" It 's not life-threatening . They 'll put up with it . "

1088
01:06:24,106 --> 01:06:26,525
- ( Skerritt ) The clinking of the ... - ( Cartwright ) Yeah , the chains and the ...

1089
01:06:26,692 --> 01:06:28,694
( Skerritt ) Oh , yeah .

1090
01:06:28,860 --> 01:06:30,529
Oh , Ridley . Ridley , Ridley .

1091
01:06:30,696 --> 01:06:34,783
( Cartwright ) And the rain . See , like , there 's all that moisture that 's coming down .

1092
01:06:34,950 --> 01:06:38,537
You wouldn 't get that moisture in outer space , I don 't think , but ...

1093
01:06:38,704 --> 01:06:40,122
( Skerritt ) You do now .

1094
01:06:40,289 --> 01:06:42,416
( Stanton ) That was my idea . Ridley loved me for that one .

1095
01:06:42,583 --> 01:06:46,461
- He lapped it up immediately . - ( Skerritt ) Yeah .

1096
01:06:53,927 --> 01:06:58,473
( Scott ) There 's always this sense that " Had I made it too slow ? "

1097
01:06:58,640 --> 01:07:01,059
It isn 't slow . I think it makes it more tense .

1098
01:07:01,226 --> 01:07:05,022
You know something always is gonna happen .

1099
01:07:07,232 --> 01:07:11,278
This was always amusing . I couldn 't get a reaction out of the cat .

1100
01:07:11,445 --> 01:07:12,779
So I said " I know what 'll do it . "

1101
01:07:12,946 --> 01:07:15,907
So I put a board alongside Harry Dean Stanton

1102
01:07:16,074 --> 01:07:20,329
and had a German shepherd there , and we just lifted the board .

1103
01:07:20,495 --> 01:07:22,623
It was on a leash so it never harmed the cat .

1104
01:07:22,789 --> 01:07:26,335
But that 's how you get that reaction , the cat that 's basically going " What ? "

1105
01:07:26,501 --> 01:07:27,961
And there .

1106
01:07:29,087 --> 01:07:31,923
That 's where it sees the shepherd .

1107
01:07:32,633 --> 01:07:35,927
And Harry 's trying to ignore it .

1108
01:07:36,637 --> 01:07:41,475
Would I do this today ? Not really . I 'd be ...

1109
01:07:41,642 --> 01:07:43,977
You know , I 'd still be going for the tension ,

1110
01:07:44,144 --> 01:07:46,605
I 'd still be going for nothing happening ,

1111
01:07:46,772 --> 01:07:50,651
but I think it works pretty well .

1112
01:07:51,985 --> 01:07:56,323
Sometimes you look at these scenes and think " I wanna cut . I wanna cut a little bit . "

1113
01:08:15,592 --> 01:08:18,970
( Skerritt ) I think the only direction we got from Ridley

1114
01:08:19,137 --> 01:08:23,475
was after we did one of those ensemble pieces in the dining room .

1115
01:08:23,642 --> 01:08:28,939
He took his eye off the camera and he winced and he said " Interesting . "

1116
01:08:29,940 --> 01:08:30,982
Do you remember that ?

1117
01:08:31,149 --> 01:08:33,860
That was the only direction he ever gave us , I believe .

1118
01:08:34,027 --> 01:08:35,112
( Stanton ) Which was enough .

1119
01:08:35,278 --> 01:08:36,613
( commentators laugh )

1120
01:08:40,867 --> 01:08:45,414
( Rawlings ) It 's very hard to talk about editing . That 's why I say this is very difficult .

1121
01:08:45,539 --> 01:08:49,251
Maybe you 'll find other people who can talk about it far more fluid than I can .

1122
01:08:49,418 --> 01:08:55,173
But whenever I 've talked about editing , people say " Why did you do so-and-so ? "

1123
01:08:55,340 --> 01:08:58,051
I have to say it was instinctive .

1124
01:08:58,218 --> 01:09:00,637
I did it because it felt right at the time .

1125
01:09:00,804 --> 01:09:03,223
It 's like if you 're teaching somebody how to do something .

1126
01:09:03,390 --> 01:09:04,850
You can 't teach people how to edit .

1127
01:09:05,016 --> 01:09:07,561
You can tell them what they can 't or they shouldn 't do ,

1128
01:09:07,728 --> 01:09:10,313
but then those rules are already there to be broken

1129
01:09:10,480 --> 01:09:13,942
because we take tremendous gambles with editing .

1130
01:09:14,109 --> 01:09:17,404
And I 'm still - after all these years of doing it -

1131
01:09:17,571 --> 01:09:20,157
endlessly surprised at what we can achieve

1132
01:09:20,323 --> 01:09:23,410
with the same pieces of film played in different ways

1133
01:09:23,577 --> 01:09:26,329
and tricked around .

1134
01:09:26,496 --> 01:09:28,540
But saying why and how you do things ,

1135
01:09:28,707 --> 01:09:31,960
it 's got to come down to the material you were given -

1136
01:09:32,544 --> 01:09:34,629
and obviously what you were trying to achieve -

1137
01:09:34,796 --> 01:09:40,218
and then it 's gotta be an in-built thing . It 's gotta be your inner timing .

1138
01:09:40,385 --> 01:09:43,472
And then of course , that will work to a certain extent

1139
01:09:43,638 --> 01:09:47,100
and then your director will suggest

1140
01:09:47,517 --> 01:09:49,436
changes which you never thought of ,

1141
01:09:49,603 --> 01:09:51,855
and I hope that 's what I do for them .

1142
01:09:52,022 --> 01:09:55,066
Is that , with all the pressures that a director has ,

1143
01:09:55,233 --> 01:10:01,072
he doesn 't have time to think of every variation on a scene .

1144
01:10:01,239 --> 01:10:03,784
I spend a lot of time talking to directors when they 're shooting

1145
01:10:03,950 --> 01:10:06,203
so I know what they 're looking for for the sequence .

1146
01:10:06,369 --> 01:10:08,330
So that when I come to put it together ,

1147
01:10:08,497 --> 01:10:12,334
the first thing I do is to put it together with the takes that they like ,

1148
01:10:12,501 --> 01:10:15,420
with the ideas that they want me to try and get across .

1149
01:10:15,587 --> 01:10:21,176
But in doing that , I see other things , and I hopefully bring that to their attention .

1150
01:10:21,343 --> 01:10:26,681
Or I do it that way so that they can see what other ideas there are .

1151
01:10:26,848 --> 01:10:28,183
And I think that 's what my job is ,

1152
01:10:28,350 --> 01:10:30,811
to sort of take what they give me a stage further .

1153
01:10:30,977 --> 01:10:33,605
Then we develop it together .

1154
01:10:44,115 --> 01:10:46,034
( Scott ) Again , this is all industrial tubing and stuff .

1155
01:10:46,201 --> 01:10:48,912
None of this we made . You know , we just got the tubing .

1156
01:10:49,079 --> 01:10:53,583
This whole thing from here , with off-cuts to them , is in a day .

1157
01:10:53,750 --> 01:10:55,335
That 's all I was given , a day .

1158
01:10:55,502 --> 01:10:57,879
" You have a day and that 's that . "

1159
01:10:58,046 --> 01:11:03,593
And I 'm shooting with a real flame and a guy holding a light , lighting himself .

1160
01:11:03,760 --> 01:11:05,887
There 's no other light in here but that . We just shot .

1161
01:11:06,054 --> 01:11:08,098
That 's always great about Derek , even on anamorphic ,

1162
01:11:08,265 --> 01:11:12,477
I just shot this as it is . That 's it . There 's nothing else there .

1163
01:11:12,644 --> 01:11:16,273
I still think Derek used to squeeze more out of an anamorphic lens

1164
01:11:16,439 --> 01:11:18,733
than anyone else , you know ?

1165
01:11:19,818 --> 01:11:24,155
But , you know , the advantage as an operator when you 're a director is ...

1166
01:11:24,322 --> 01:11:27,158
Because you 're seeing it , you know you 're getting it .

1167
01:11:27,325 --> 01:11:28,910
You know , you know what you 're getting .

1168
01:11:29,077 --> 01:11:31,580
So it 's almost like putting the storyboard together in your head

1169
01:11:31,746 --> 01:11:33,582
as you 're seeing it happen .

1170
01:11:33,748 --> 01:11:35,458
If you 're not looking through the viewfinder ,

1171
01:11:35,625 --> 01:11:37,752
you 're kind of insulated from what 's going on .

1172
01:11:37,919 --> 01:11:40,380
I mean , we 're helped today by good video assist and everything ,

1173
01:11:40,547 --> 01:11:43,008
but there 's nothing quite like being through the viewfinder

1174
01:11:43,174 --> 01:11:46,261
and being close to the actor . You 're very much part of it .

1175
01:11:47,512 --> 01:11:52,434
To me , there 's an entire logic to that , of operating and directing the same thing .

1176
01:11:54,519 --> 01:11:56,229
( Shusett ) I remember when Ridley met me , he said :

1177
01:11:56,396 --> 01:12:01,026
" I want it to be the most straightforward , unpretentious , riveting thriller ,

1178
01:12:01,192 --> 01:12:03,987
like Psycho or Rosemary 's Baby ,

1179
01:12:04,154 --> 01:12:07,991
or even the most brilliant B-level , like Night of the Living Dead

1180
01:12:08,158 --> 01:12:10,035
and Texas Chain Saw Massacre . "

1181
01:12:10,201 --> 01:12:14,331
" But I want it to look - and I 'm going to do this - like 2001 . "

1182
01:12:14,497 --> 01:12:16,708
And I knew right then - because I 'd seen The Duellists -

1183
01:12:16,875 --> 01:12:18,627
I knew he could make it look like 2001 ,

1184
01:12:18,793 --> 01:12:21,046
but I didn 't realize that he had that frame of mind .

1185
01:12:21,212 --> 01:12:23,089
" I 'm not gonna make this into a pretentious ... "

1186
01:12:23,256 --> 01:12:25,258
" I want it to play just like it read on the page . "

1187
01:12:25,425 --> 01:12:28,929
And then he said " We 're gonna watch together all the classic scare movies

1188
01:12:29,095 --> 01:12:31,389
so I can get the rhythm of how scares work . "

1189
01:12:31,556 --> 01:12:34,559
And we did that . We made a list and we all - Ridley and I and Dan O 'Bannon -

1190
01:12:34,726 --> 01:12:37,270
we all watched them . And I knew from when he even said that -

1191
01:12:37,437 --> 01:12:41,691
from that first meeting - I knew that he was gonna do just the job he did .

1192
01:12:43,944 --> 01:12:46,655
( Scott ) I was a bit nervous about these flamethrowers .

1193
01:12:46,821 --> 01:12:48,323
We were assured they were safe -

1194
01:12:48,490 --> 01:12:51,159
and of course the actors were taken off to practice with them -

1195
01:12:51,326 --> 01:12:52,869
but I was always nervous about them .

1196
01:12:53,036 --> 01:12:56,623
The liquid fuel and ... I didn 't like them at all .

1197
01:12:56,790 --> 01:12:59,417
We were very careful and cautious about everything .

1198
01:12:59,584 --> 01:13:04,089
So fortunately , there were no accidents , but I do not like playing with fire .

1199
01:13:05,757 --> 01:13:08,176
( O 'Bannon ) I was stuck on one point ,

1200
01:13:08,343 --> 01:13:11,596
which was once they got the thing on the spaceship ,

1201
01:13:11,763 --> 01:13:15,725
I wanted to avoid the cliché of bullets bouncing off of the thing -

1202
01:13:15,892 --> 01:13:17,143
the indestructible monster .

1203
01:13:17,310 --> 01:13:19,729
I mean , that 's the ancient cliché , right ?

1204
01:13:19,896 --> 01:13:23,108
You can 't stop it , bullets won 't stop it . Not at all .

1205
01:13:23,274 --> 01:13:27,028
I wanted the thing to be , in every respect , a natural animal .

1206
01:13:27,195 --> 01:13:30,073
Which means , yes , if you shoot it , it 'll die .

1207
01:13:30,240 --> 01:13:33,535
So the question was , in the second half of the movie :

1208
01:13:33,702 --> 01:13:37,372
" Why don 't they just kill the thing ? Why don 't they just squash it ? " Right ?

1209
01:13:37,539 --> 01:13:39,958
" Stick a knife in it , whatever . "

1210
01:13:40,125 --> 01:13:41,960
And I wasn 't sure how to achieve that

1211
01:13:42,127 --> 01:13:45,338
and I 'd asked Ron Cobb if he had any thoughts .

1212
01:13:45,505 --> 01:13:49,134
Ron Cobb , I remember - who was always helpful - said :

1213
01:13:49,300 --> 01:13:55,348
" Well , suppose the thing bled acid that would , like , burn through metal . "

1214
01:13:55,515 --> 01:13:59,644
I said " Great . " I said " Then they couldn 't kill it cos then it would ... "

1215
01:13:59,811 --> 01:14:03,231
" Its blood would eat a hole in the bulkhead and the ship would lose all of its oxygen . "

1216
01:14:03,398 --> 01:14:05,400
I said " Great . "

1217
01:14:05,900 --> 01:14:08,862
( Skerritt ) Oh , look at this . This is the other guy gets it .

1218
01:14:09,029 --> 01:14:11,448
This damn fool .

1219
01:14:11,614 --> 01:14:16,619
" Okay , Harry , order me a beer . I think I 'm gonna join you soon . "

1220
01:14:16,786 --> 01:14:17,871
( Stanton chuckles )

1221
01:14:25,795 --> 01:14:28,631
- ( Cartwright ) Ew . - ( Skerritt ) Oh , yeah .

1222
01:14:29,507 --> 01:14:32,635
He sang me a ballad . That 's what it was .

1223
01:14:32,802 --> 01:14:35,388
That 's why I couldn 't shoot him .

1224
01:14:36,181 --> 01:14:39,350
( Scott ) It 's funny , they always used to amuse the hell out of me , because you 'd see ...

1225
01:14:39,517 --> 01:14:41,603
The whole audience would ...

1226
01:14:41,770 --> 01:14:44,898
When you get a nice bump of 800 people all together ,

1227
01:14:45,065 --> 01:14:46,733
that really is gratifying .

1228
01:14:46,900 --> 01:14:51,696
It almost becomes really amusing , actually . But it is fun to do a thriller .

1229
01:14:51,863 --> 01:14:57,327
I 'd like to do it again , but I need to find the piece of material .

1230
01:14:57,494 --> 01:14:59,496
It doesn 't have to be that sophisticated , you know ?

1231
01:14:59,662 --> 01:15:02,832
You can make it more sophisticated , really .

1232
01:15:07,212 --> 01:15:09,214
- ( Cartwright ) See , I am the only smart one . - ( Stanton ) Yeah .

1233
01:15:09,380 --> 01:15:11,716
( Cartwright ) We abandon this stupid ship and get the hell out of here .

1234
01:15:11,883 --> 01:15:12,926
( Skerritt ) The audience is right with you .

1235
01:15:13,093 --> 01:15:14,886
( Cartwright ) Nobody listens . Yeah , nobody listens .

1236
01:15:15,053 --> 01:15:16,304
( Skerritt ) The audience is cheering you on .

1237
01:15:16,471 --> 01:15:18,890
( Stanton ) Why didn 't you say that before they got to me ? Yeah .

1238
01:15:19,057 --> 01:15:20,100
( Stanton chuckles )

1239
01:15:20,266 --> 01:15:21,559
Why didn 't you bring it up earlier ?

1240
01:15:21,726 --> 01:15:24,896
( Skerritt ) Well , that 's cos ... Whatever my character ...

1241
01:15:25,063 --> 01:15:29,234
Dallas and her were having an affair , and that 's why she wanted to ...

1242
01:15:29,400 --> 01:15:30,777
- ( Stanton ) Yeah . - ( Cartwright ) Which they cut out .

1243
01:15:30,944 --> 01:15:33,071
( Stanton ) She was getting laid .

1244
01:15:33,363 --> 01:15:36,241
( Skerritt ) So there you have it .

1245
01:15:36,950 --> 01:15:42,330
( Scott ) This is the bit where I wanted to raise the level a little bit .

1246
01:15:42,497 --> 01:15:46,376
So Yaphet and I were a little complicit here ,

1247
01:15:46,543 --> 01:15:51,506
and said " I wanna crank the volume up here and push him , and be irritating . "

1248
01:15:51,673 --> 01:15:54,592
And we got it . It was very good .

1249
01:15:54,759 --> 01:15:56,052
( Cartwright ) And I know that ...

1250
01:15:56,219 --> 01:15:59,013
I mean , because she started out being very emotional

1251
01:15:59,180 --> 01:16:01,141
and all of this stuff because of Dallas dying ,

1252
01:16:01,307 --> 01:16:04,686
and he just kept pushing her and pushing her , and pushing her .

1253
01:16:04,853 --> 01:16:08,231
And I know Ridley was saying " Get to her gut . "

1254
01:16:08,398 --> 01:16:10,066
So when he gave that whole speech and he slams down his thing ,

1255
01:16:11,317 --> 01:16:14,654
and he walks off the set again , she was so pissed off ,

1256
01:16:14,821 --> 01:16:16,072
which was what the movie needed .

1257
01:16:16,239 --> 01:16:19,534
I mean , she had to take control of that ship .

1258
01:16:19,701 --> 01:16:22,871
She couldn 't be a wimpy person . I was the wimp .

1259
01:16:23,037 --> 01:16:25,874
You know , I was the only sane one . But I think he did egg us on .

1260
01:16:26,040 --> 01:16:27,333
( Cartwright laughs )

1261
01:16:27,792 --> 01:16:29,627
( Scott ) Relationships would be discouraged .

1262
01:16:29,794 --> 01:16:33,298
You know , the idea of casual sex would be normal ,

1263
01:16:33,464 --> 01:16:36,050
for obvious reasons . I thought " Why not ? "

1264
01:16:36,217 --> 01:16:38,553
Because if you 've got seven on board , somebody 's gonna get left out .

1265
01:16:38,720 --> 01:16:41,264
And so casual relationships , whether it 's male or female ,

1266
01:16:41,431 --> 01:16:43,725
male with male , female with female ,

1267
01:16:43,892 --> 01:16:47,228
seems to be okay in space .

1268
01:16:47,395 --> 01:16:53,401
When you 're locked away in a big tin can for years on end ,

1269
01:16:53,568 --> 01:16:57,739
could be years , plus hyper-sleep , so it might feel like a year .

1270
01:16:57,906 --> 01:16:59,199
You might be away 10 years .

1271
01:16:59,365 --> 01:17:03,870
So I tried to instigate that and there was a suggestion of that with Dallas .

1272
01:17:04,037 --> 01:17:08,249
And that was the beginning with Veronica .

1273
01:17:08,458 --> 01:17:12,921
Say , an idea of " Should we infer something here ? "

1274
01:17:13,087 --> 01:17:19,594
" Should we have an inference of , you know , a lesbian or gay relationship , or not ? "

1275
01:17:19,761 --> 01:17:22,472
It would have been kind of interesting .

1276
01:17:22,639 --> 01:17:29,854
Today I 'd probably do that just to thicken up the layers in the characters .

1277
01:17:30,021 --> 01:17:33,441
( O 'Bannon ) The general idea of what constitutes a suspense story

1278
01:17:33,608 --> 01:17:37,654
was an issue of some contention among the producers ,

1279
01:17:37,820 --> 01:17:39,447
and I lost a couple of those battles .

1280
01:17:40,823 --> 01:17:44,035
There was no Ash in my original script , they added that .

1281
01:17:44,202 --> 01:17:47,914
The idea being here that all scripts must have a subplot .

1282
01:17:48,081 --> 01:17:52,585
Simply to have a single plot by itself is inadequate ,

1283
01:17:52,752 --> 01:17:56,798
all stories must have subplots . So they created a subplot .

1284
01:17:56,965 --> 01:18:01,803
Ian Holm gives a brilliant performance , it 's brilliantly directed by Ridley ,

1285
01:18:02,262 --> 01:18:04,222
but if you stop and think about it ,

1286
01:18:04,389 --> 01:18:07,308
if it wasn 't in there , what difference would it make one way or the other ?

1287
01:18:07,475 --> 01:18:10,812
I mean , who gives a rat 's ass ? I mean , so somebody is a robot .

1288
01:18:10,979 --> 01:18:16,317
It annoyed me when they did it because it was what I called the Russian spy .

1289
01:18:16,484 --> 01:18:18,361
It was a tendency in certain types of thrillers -

1290
01:18:18,528 --> 01:18:23,408
when people are on an interesting mission - to stick in a Russian spy .

1291
01:18:23,574 --> 01:18:25,827
One of them is a spy and they don 't know which one

1292
01:18:25,994 --> 01:18:29,247
and he 's trying to screw up the mission . Fantastic Voyage had that .

1293
01:18:29,414 --> 01:18:32,917
When I saw Fantastic Voyage , I found it annoying .

1294
01:18:33,084 --> 01:18:38,131
You 're just about getting ready to head off into the body of this person

1295
01:18:38,298 --> 01:18:39,590
and have this fantastic mission

1296
01:18:39,757 --> 01:18:42,969
to go through his bloodstream , get to the brain and save him ,

1297
01:18:43,136 --> 01:18:46,014
when you 're informed that one of them is a Russian spy

1298
01:18:46,180 --> 01:18:49,934
and he 's going to stop the mission from its completion .

1299
01:18:50,101 --> 01:18:52,353
And instead of it adding any genuine suspense ,

1300
01:18:52,520 --> 01:18:54,230
all it did was annoy me and made me think :

1301
01:18:54,397 --> 01:18:58,401
" Oh , I see . So maybe now I don 't get to see what I wanna see in the movie

1302
01:18:58,568 --> 01:19:00,695
because the Russian spy will prevent it . "

1303
01:19:00,862 --> 01:19:06,159
It 's a tensioning device which is commonly resorted to and doesn 't work ,

1304
01:19:06,326 --> 01:19:08,369
cos it doesn 't provide any real suspense .

1305
01:19:08,536 --> 01:19:14,042
It doesn 't do anything , except provide finger exercise for the writer

1306
01:19:14,208 --> 01:19:16,711
who thinks that all stories must have subplots .

1307
01:19:16,878 --> 01:19:20,715
So I think it 's an inferior idea , of inferior minds ,

1308
01:19:20,882 --> 01:19:22,675
well-acted and well-directed .

1309
01:19:22,842 --> 01:19:26,804
And fortunately , it occupies little enough screen time

1310
01:19:26,971 --> 01:19:29,640
that it doesn 't disrupt the main plot .

1311
01:19:29,807 --> 01:19:31,726
( Scott ) The Ash thing was interesting .

1312
01:19:31,893 --> 01:19:36,356
That Ash was an implant of the corporation - having a robot on board .

1313
01:19:36,522 --> 01:19:41,444
So instead of just having a spy , you 've got a biomechanoid human being .

1314
01:19:41,611 --> 01:19:42,779
( Weaver ) It 's a very modern idea , still very relevant .

1315
01:19:42,987 --> 01:19:44,197
( Scott ) Yeah , very modern .

1316
01:19:44,530 --> 01:19:47,533
He has a formidable memory so he doesn 't even need a computer .

1317
01:19:47,700 --> 01:19:50,370
It all goes in and it 'll all spout out later .

1318
01:19:50,536 --> 01:19:52,372
I thought that was a great ... That was a first .

1319
01:19:52,538 --> 01:19:54,999
- ( Weaver ) Yeah . Yes , yes , yes . - ( Scott ) I thought , that 's a really good idea .

1320
01:19:55,166 --> 01:19:56,876
It makes sense .

1321
01:19:57,919 --> 01:20:02,715
( Weaver ) You said " Well , come on downstairs . It 's gonna be great . "

1322
01:20:02,882 --> 01:20:08,262
" Ash is gonna pick up this sex magazine and he 's gonna stick it up your hooter . "

1323
01:20:08,429 --> 01:20:13,393
And I didn 't know cockney , and I thought " Hm , my hooter ? "

1324
01:20:13,559 --> 01:20:16,437
And so luckily , when we got downstairs ,

1325
01:20:16,604 --> 01:20:17,647
( Weaver laughing )

1326
01:20:17,814 --> 01:20:19,732
it was up my mouth to choke me .

1327
01:20:19,899 --> 01:20:24,237
But it was funny . It was one of those things . You know , I didn 't actually think ...

1328
01:20:24,404 --> 01:20:28,408
- " Well , Ridley would never do that . " - ( Scott ) Exactly .

1329
01:20:28,574 --> 01:20:29,909
( Weaver ) Nothing would have surprised me .

1330
01:20:30,076 --> 01:20:32,161
( Scott ) Exactly . I figured robots had to have ...

1331
01:20:32,328 --> 01:20:37,041
If they 're really sophisticated , had to occasionally have the urge .

1332
01:20:37,208 --> 01:20:39,585
So I 'd said to Ash " How do you feel about sexual drive ? "

1333
01:20:39,752 --> 01:20:41,254
He said " Great . "

1334
01:20:41,421 --> 01:20:42,588
( Weaver laughs )

1335
01:20:42,755 --> 01:20:46,884
So I said " Rather than just beating her up , isn 't it more interesting

1336
01:20:47,051 --> 01:20:51,556
that he actually has always wanted to

1337
01:20:51,722 --> 01:20:55,268
and here 's his opportunity , but he doesn 't have that part . "

1338
01:20:55,435 --> 01:20:57,770
- ( Weaver ) Oh , he doesn 't . - ( Scott ) " And therefore it 's a magazine . "

1339
01:20:57,937 --> 01:21:02,191
( Weaver ) Ah . I didn 't understand the Freudian overtones of the scene .

1340
01:21:02,358 --> 01:21:04,444
( Scott ) I hope there aren 't any kids listening to all this .

1341
01:21:04,610 --> 01:21:07,989
( Weaver ) Well , if kids can watch these movies , they can hear this stuff .

1342
01:21:08,156 --> 01:21:09,866
( Scott ) Exactly .

1343
01:21:10,616 --> 01:21:13,286
( Shusett ) This is Giler and Hill 's concept .

1344
01:21:13,786 --> 01:21:17,999
It was the only thing that wasn 't in the original script of Dan 's and mine .

1345
01:21:18,166 --> 01:21:20,209
And the reason I knew it would work ...

1346
01:21:20,376 --> 01:21:22,712
Because you needed something after the chestburster

1347
01:21:22,879 --> 01:21:25,923
that was at least close to it - as amazing - and that is this ,

1348
01:21:26,090 --> 01:21:29,260
when his head is knocked off and you don 't realize he 's a robot .

1349
01:21:33,931 --> 01:21:37,477
And that kept the third act from being a letdown .

1350
01:21:39,312 --> 01:21:42,940
Oh , God . Jesus Christ .

1351
01:21:43,357 --> 01:21:46,402
( Scott ) This is a great turnabout in the story

1352
01:21:46,569 --> 01:21:52,116
because , really , just when you think that your main and only aggressor

1353
01:21:52,283 --> 01:21:55,661
is this thing loose on the ship ,

1354
01:21:57,163 --> 01:21:59,749
you now got a much bigger problem .

1355
01:21:59,916 --> 01:22:02,585
You 've got two aggressors .

1356
01:22:02,752 --> 01:22:08,674
Which raises their paranoia , and that of the audience , twofold .

1357
01:22:09,383 --> 01:22:14,013
Always useful at this moment , when you 're about three quarters of the way through .

1358
01:22:14,180 --> 01:22:17,099
So also , suddenly the drops of milk made logic .

1359
01:22:17,266 --> 01:22:20,895
Cos instead of having spurting blood or fluid , I decided to have spurting ...

1360
01:22:21,062 --> 01:22:23,147
I didn 't use milk , because it would stink in no time .

1361
01:22:23,314 --> 01:22:26,234
So it was just basically colored water .

1362
01:22:27,527 --> 01:22:30,780
But considering all things

1363
01:22:31,489 --> 01:22:34,200
and the budget involved , we did pretty good .

1364
01:22:35,451 --> 01:22:37,578
This was again " What do we wanna do for the innards ? "

1365
01:22:37,745 --> 01:22:43,334
And I said " Pasta , glass marbles ... "

1366
01:22:43,501 --> 01:22:46,754
" Get me , not caviar , but get me a bit of the cheap caviar ,

1367
01:22:46,921 --> 01:22:48,756
and milk , and that 's it . "

1368
01:22:48,923 --> 01:22:52,927
And I just dressed it on the table . This is years of pack shots , you see .

1369
01:22:53,094 --> 01:22:57,014
It 's some pasta ... Thin rubber tubes , pasta ,

1370
01:22:57,181 --> 01:23:00,518
and there 's a cheap form of black caviar which was great .

1371
01:23:00,768 --> 01:23:02,562
Dress that with the glass balls and you think :

1372
01:23:02,728 --> 01:23:05,773
" My God , that 's so high-tech , I don 't know what it is . "

1373
01:23:06,732 --> 01:23:10,778
But it just shows , you don 't need that much . You know , you don 't need to go nuts .

1374
01:23:11,237 --> 01:23:14,824
( Cartwright ) The original scene had more grapey things and stuff ,

1375
01:23:14,991 --> 01:23:16,826
and so I guess they took it ...

1376
01:23:16,993 --> 01:23:19,662
I talked to Ian later and he said they went back and reshot it

1377
01:23:19,829 --> 01:23:23,958
with more tubey looking odds and ends .

1378
01:23:24,125 --> 01:23:25,376
And they also changed the dialogue .

1379
01:23:26,836 --> 01:23:29,005
It wasn 't what it was originally .

1380
01:23:29,171 --> 01:23:30,965
Well , that whole thing about how :

1381
01:23:31,132 --> 01:23:33,467
" Well , nobody had bothered to try to communicate with it . "

1382
01:23:33,634 --> 01:23:36,971
" I mean , maybe if we gave it a chance ... " " It was part of an experimental program ... "

1383
01:23:37,138 --> 01:23:40,641
Which in a weird sort of way didn 't make him as evil .

1384
01:23:41,851 --> 01:23:45,354
Originally , this is where he had brought up :

1385
01:23:45,521 --> 01:23:48,107
" Has anybody tried to communicate with it ? "

1386
01:23:48,274 --> 01:23:51,611
And we were all standing around and ... You know , and listening to him .

1387
01:23:51,777 --> 01:23:54,322
And he was so touching when he was doing it .

1388
01:23:54,488 --> 01:23:56,490
And then Ridley shouts " cut "

1389
01:23:56,657 --> 01:23:59,493
because he had milk and he had grapes and he had little ...

1390
01:23:59,660 --> 01:24:04,373
He hated the little silver balls that were , like , on the c ...

1391
01:24:04,540 --> 01:24:08,210
So here we are , we 're all , like , sitting there with bated breath listening to Ian

1392
01:24:08,377 --> 01:24:10,129
who 's got his head in the middle of a table ,

1393
01:24:10,296 --> 01:24:13,174
you know , with grapes and all sorts of stuff hanging off his head ,

1394
01:24:13,341 --> 01:24:15,468
and then he ...

1395
01:24:16,093 --> 01:24:18,929
He shouts " cut " because he didn 't like the silver balls .

1396
01:24:19,096 --> 01:24:23,684
So what you see is , Ian went back months later and redid it .

1397
01:24:23,851 --> 01:24:26,103
But I loved his ideas .

1398
01:24:26,270 --> 01:24:30,858
Ian had this twitch throughout the thing , which you don 't get to see very much .

1399
01:24:31,025 --> 01:24:36,280
He had , like ... He starts out fine , but as he starts to get ...

1400
01:24:36,447 --> 01:24:43,162
This left eye would , like , twitch all the time as he starts to break down .

1401
01:24:46,666 --> 01:24:48,918
( Scott ) So now we 're probably trying to work out all ...

1402
01:24:49,085 --> 01:24:54,465
You know , you don 't have to explain it , that craft have droids on board .

1403
01:24:54,632 --> 01:24:56,676
You know , big corporations ...

1404
01:24:56,842 --> 01:25:00,513
Maybe the rumor has always been from the big corporations do ...

1405
01:25:00,680 --> 01:25:03,099
Out of paranoia for their own investment

1406
01:25:03,265 --> 01:25:05,685
of the huge craft and the cargo and the knowledge ,

1407
01:25:05,851 --> 01:25:08,229
always plant a spy within the crew on board

1408
01:25:08,396 --> 01:25:14,568
just in case the crew decided to go off and sell it to somebody .

1409
01:25:14,735 --> 01:25:19,240
And therefore , they always have their own security blanket as part of the crew .

1410
01:25:19,407 --> 01:25:23,744
And he , of course , is one . And I think that was a really nice idea -

1411
01:25:23,911 --> 01:25:29,917
a new idea - which then gets used again and again and again and again , you know .

1412
01:25:32,086 --> 01:25:36,090
( Shusett ) And Sigourney was a revelation to us because on the set ,

1413
01:25:36,257 --> 01:25:39,885
she never seemed to be acting much because she 's reflective .

1414
01:25:40,052 --> 01:25:41,595
It 's all behind the eyes .

1415
01:25:41,762 --> 01:25:45,558
These guys are , all of them , surrounded by the best character actors in the business

1416
01:25:45,725 --> 01:25:49,562
and we thought " Gee , is she gonna be any good ? She 's not acting . "

1417
01:25:49,729 --> 01:25:52,273
And every time we 'd look at the dailies , we 'd say " She 's great . "

1418
01:25:52,440 --> 01:25:55,776
" How come she looks so great ? " Because it was always ... It 's behind the eyes .

1419
01:25:55,943 --> 01:25:57,069
As Orson Welles once said ...

1420
01:25:57,236 --> 01:25:58,946
When he was watching Gary Cooper shoot a scene on the set , he said :

1421
01:26:00,364 --> 01:26:03,868
" Oh , we 'll have to reshoot that . " Then he looked at the dailies and it was fantastic .

1422
01:26:04,034 --> 01:26:07,288
Orson Welles said " That camera either loves you or it doesn 't . "

1423
01:26:07,455 --> 01:26:13,252
And Sigourney 's whole career and success is she doesn 't overact it .

1424
01:26:14,295 --> 01:26:19,049
( O 'Bannon ) The whole point of Alien isn 't that this is a dangerous space monster .

1425
01:26:19,216 --> 01:26:23,179
The whole point of Alien - according to Walter Hill -

1426
01:26:23,345 --> 01:26:27,558
is that evil corporations created this situation .

1427
01:26:27,725 --> 01:26:31,353
This crew wouldn 't even be in this desperate situation in the first place

1428
01:26:31,520 --> 01:26:35,775
if the evil corporation hadn 't sought out this organism

1429
01:26:35,941 --> 01:26:37,943
and decided to use it as a weapon ,

1430
01:26:38,110 --> 01:26:41,822
and stuck a robot on board to deceive the crew .

1431
01:26:41,989 --> 01:26:49,246
And get them trapped in a situation where this alien organism could do its worst

1432
01:26:49,413 --> 01:26:52,291
and show that it would be a very good weapon

1433
01:26:52,458 --> 01:26:54,710
for the corporation 's weapon systems .

1434
01:26:54,877 --> 01:26:58,547
And as far as Walter Hill is concerned , that 's what the movie is about .

1435
01:26:59,381 --> 01:27:04,970
But you can see how , having taken the time and trouble

1436
01:27:05,137 --> 01:27:08,724
to have conceived of and write Alien in the first place ,

1437
01:27:08,891 --> 01:27:13,771
to find myself butting up against a producer who was a writer .

1438
01:27:14,313 --> 01:27:17,358
( Scott ) This was anamorphic . I wanted a wide screen .

1439
01:27:17,525 --> 01:27:21,403
Perceptive , a wide screen is ... Feels like it 's bigger .

1440
01:27:21,570 --> 01:27:26,492
Then gradually they developed into the idea of Super 35 , which is ...

1441
01:27:26,659 --> 01:27:29,453
The big difference with anamorphic is you 're using anamorphic lenses ,

1442
01:27:29,620 --> 01:27:32,581
which means there 's more glass on the front of the lens ,

1443
01:27:32,748 --> 01:27:37,044
which means that the picture quality is not as sharp .

1444
01:27:37,211 --> 01:27:39,797
It means you need more light to push through more glass .

1445
01:27:39,964 --> 01:27:43,884
So it sounds like all downside .

1446
01:27:44,051 --> 01:27:46,887
And in these days , it was , cos we had a lot of focus problems in this

1447
01:27:47,054 --> 01:27:50,599
because we were kind of low-light level , right on the edge .

1448
01:27:50,766 --> 01:27:53,561
So that means your low-light level , being right on the edge ,

1449
01:27:53,727 --> 01:27:58,399
you 're approaching wide open , which is not good for the lens and clarity .

1450
01:27:58,566 --> 01:28:01,151
So we had a bit of focus problem .

1451
01:28:01,318 --> 01:28:05,489
You know , you can use the loss of depth of focus on anamorphic

1452
01:28:05,656 --> 01:28:08,200
which is a thing in itself - it 's part of the characterization

1453
01:28:08,367 --> 01:28:12,204
of the kind of style you 're choosing and the story you 're telling .

1454
01:28:12,371 --> 01:28:16,083
Just the remains of a helicopter there , just sprayed gold .

1455
01:28:16,250 --> 01:28:20,880
Jet engines there , turned on end and sprayed gold . With gold foil on them ,

1456
01:28:21,046 --> 01:28:26,176
just to make it look more peculiarly high-tech .

1457
01:28:26,343 --> 01:28:29,722
This is where you get the nice flares on the glass like that , from anamorphic .

1458
01:28:31,682 --> 01:28:34,810
Again , silences , silences .

1459
01:28:35,769 --> 01:28:40,190
Being with the actress or the actor ... We can literally be right there with them

1460
01:28:40,357 --> 01:28:42,985
if you 're hand-holding , you see this .

1461
01:28:47,072 --> 01:28:53,495
Veronica was always great at controlling barely controlled terror - catatonic terror .

1462
01:28:53,662 --> 01:28:55,915
She 's always , like , two steps from a heart attack ,

1463
01:28:56,081 --> 01:28:59,376
which I think she finally does at the end - have a heart attack .

1464
01:29:00,169 --> 01:29:04,048
( Rawlings ) It 's fantastic , you see , the time you take over everything .

1465
01:29:04,214 --> 01:29:07,760
This is what makes this film so special .

1466
01:29:08,218 --> 01:29:13,807
And when it happens , it 's like that . It 's over . It 's over .

1467
01:29:15,351 --> 01:29:18,771
( Scott ) Again , would I buy Jonesy today ? I didn 't even think about it in those days .

1468
01:29:18,938 --> 01:29:22,191
I thought " Why not ? You know , you have a cat , she 'd be attached to the cat . "

1469
01:29:22,358 --> 01:29:24,902
Like I 've got dogs , I 'd do anything for my dogs .

1470
01:29:25,611 --> 01:29:28,530
Would I go back for my dogs ? Absolutely .

1471
01:29:29,573 --> 01:29:33,827
The air-condition going . The little fans working .

1472
01:29:33,994 --> 01:29:39,083
So I have air lines of people blowing things , so everything 's moving and bouncing .

1473
01:29:39,249 --> 01:29:42,795
And then there 's a useful thing here .

1474
01:29:45,130 --> 01:29:48,842
Again , a useful shock is coming up .

1475
01:29:50,511 --> 01:29:52,763
That was very useful at this point .

1476
01:29:52,930 --> 01:29:56,266
And very simple , you know ?

1477
01:29:56,433 --> 01:29:58,769
Just have a guy , a propman , push it forward with his feet .

1478
01:29:59,144 --> 01:30:03,857
He 's not ... None of that ... Just somebody there pushing it forward .

1479
01:30:11,615 --> 01:30:17,705
That is a clue . The cat - meow - going inside is a clue .

1480
01:30:17,871 --> 01:30:23,043
People were convinced that the alien was now inside the cat .

1481
01:30:26,880 --> 01:30:29,800
( Shusett ) Yaphet is so good as he 's so intense .

1482
01:30:29,967 --> 01:30:32,386
He said " I 've been waiting 15 years for something like this ,

1483
01:30:32,553 --> 01:30:35,305
that I knew would become an amazing , all-time great movie . "

1484
01:30:35,472 --> 01:30:39,143
Before we even started shooting . Can you imagine that ? What great vision .

1485
01:30:39,309 --> 01:30:41,937
First day I met him , he said that to me .

1486
01:30:45,441 --> 01:30:48,736
( Cartwright ) Yaphet is the one who keeps telling me to move but I can 't really move

1487
01:30:48,902 --> 01:30:52,281
when I 'm between him and the alien , and here he comes .

1488
01:30:52,448 --> 01:30:56,618
See , now , this is the thing . He 's , like , fascinating in a weird sort of way .

1489
01:30:56,994 --> 01:30:59,788
Look at him . He 's , like , looking at me . He 's , like , checking me out .

1490
01:30:59,955 --> 01:31:05,335
So I was going off of the fact that I would end up in a locker somehow ,

1491
01:31:05,502 --> 01:31:09,965
but obviously , he 's doing other things to me .

1492
01:31:10,966 --> 01:31:14,595
Bolaji , who played the alien , he was just amazing .

1493
01:31:14,762 --> 01:31:18,348
I mean , he was this graphic artist that they found in a pub .

1494
01:31:18,515 --> 01:31:23,353
And he was 7 foot tall and he was Masai , so he had very long limbs ,

1495
01:31:23,520 --> 01:31:27,941
so actually that suit fit him . But he 'd walk around in these white sneakers .

1496
01:31:28,108 --> 01:31:31,195
And Tom was the one who actually said " This poor guy can 't sit down . "

1497
01:31:31,361 --> 01:31:33,530
And they built him a special swing .

1498
01:31:33,947 --> 01:31:37,034
Cos he couldn 't sit down once he had that tail on .

1499
01:31:37,201 --> 01:31:39,036
And I always remember Ridley wanting to do ,

1500
01:31:39,203 --> 01:31:42,247
and there is a shot of it ... You see the head and the brain ?

1501
01:31:42,414 --> 01:31:45,375
Well , that was maggots , and they wanted to stick that on Bolaji 's head

1502
01:31:45,542 --> 01:31:48,504
and he goes " I draw the line . "

1503
01:31:48,670 --> 01:31:51,131
See , now , that 's Harry Dean 's legs

1504
01:31:51,298 --> 01:31:54,301
because I wear white pants and cowboy boots through the whole thing .

1505
01:31:54,468 --> 01:31:56,345
But that was taken from the scene

1506
01:31:56,512 --> 01:32:01,391
where the alien comes down in that warehouse thing .

1507
01:32:01,558 --> 01:32:05,312
Now , they manipulated the film so they don 't look blue ,

1508
01:32:05,479 --> 01:32:10,234
but that 's not even the grating that 's on the floor .

1509
01:32:10,400 --> 01:32:12,569
I remember the first time seeing this with an audience

1510
01:32:12,736 --> 01:32:15,405
and then , of course , the reporters , they go " And how did it feel ? "

1511
01:32:15,572 --> 01:32:17,407
I said " I don 't know . Why don 't you ask Harry ? "

1512
01:32:17,574 --> 01:32:20,202
I was so pissed off .

1513
01:32:20,744 --> 01:32:26,583
I mean , years later it works , so it 's fine , but I think they could 've warned me .

1514
01:32:27,084 --> 01:32:29,753
But you see , this death scene , it was never completed originally .

1515
01:32:29,920 --> 01:32:32,089
What I was supposed to do was crawl away

1516
01:32:32,256 --> 01:32:37,052
and I basically die of fright in the locker ,

1517
01:32:37,219 --> 01:32:40,806
and it was supposedly the same locker that Jones had been in .

1518
01:32:40,973 --> 01:32:47,938
But that was never shot . And I kept asking when we were going to finish the scene ,

1519
01:32:48,105 --> 01:32:51,900
cos , I mean , the next thing I knew , they were onto something else .

1520
01:32:52,860 --> 01:32:54,444
( Scott ) I mean , we didn 't know how she died ...

1521
01:32:54,611 --> 01:32:58,240
But the implication that there was a kind of sexuality

1522
01:32:58,407 --> 01:33:03,787
to this androgynous male-female who could give birth itself ,

1523
01:33:03,954 --> 01:33:06,206
but it could also impregnate .

1524
01:33:06,373 --> 01:33:10,919
So it 's like ... There are insects like that , so we based that on ...

1525
01:33:11,086 --> 01:33:13,297
You know , a little bit of good old Mother Nature .

1526
01:33:13,463 --> 01:33:17,593
And was that some dreadful ending ?

1527
01:33:17,759 --> 01:33:21,805
Was that some terrible invasion of her body , a rape ?

1528
01:33:21,972 --> 01:33:28,103
And therefore , would there be a version of the Cartwright character ?

1529
01:33:28,270 --> 01:33:33,400
Certainly whatever happens , there 'd be more humanoid aliens now on board this craft ,

1530
01:33:33,567 --> 01:33:36,403
and that 's what she 's now gotta destroy .

1531
01:33:36,612 --> 01:33:41,283
That 's one of the difficulties . You now go again into this genre

1532
01:33:41,450 --> 01:33:48,874
and then think of something that is , you know , equally unique , and it 's difficult .

1533
01:33:49,875 --> 01:33:53,295
You know , I just fell on Giger at these moments ,

1534
01:33:53,462 --> 01:33:57,966
who hadn 't been seen that much except in Switzerland .

1535
01:33:58,133 --> 01:34:02,763
Had a following actually at that moment , but he was just perfect .

1536
01:34:02,930 --> 01:34:06,016
The , you know , right elements came together at the right time .

1537
01:34:06,642 --> 01:34:09,811
( Cartwright ) If you think about it , everybody who 's done a horror movie after this

1538
01:34:09,978 --> 01:34:13,190
has copied what the alien looks like .

1539
01:34:13,357 --> 01:34:14,775
The only one that hasn 't

1540
01:34:15,067 --> 01:34:18,695
really looked like this was in Signs as an alien .

1541
01:34:18,862 --> 01:34:23,116
But it 's still that elongated sort of body .

1542
01:34:23,283 --> 01:34:27,037
But if you think about it , all of these movies , they 've ...

1543
01:34:27,204 --> 01:34:32,793
This obviously is a classic , and they 've all sort of followed this theory .

1544
01:34:33,126 --> 01:34:36,880
This was the first time that it wasn 't clean , I think .

1545
01:34:37,047 --> 01:34:40,133
All those outer-space movies were so clean .

1546
01:34:40,300 --> 01:34:43,512
And this isn 't clean . It 's dirty and it 's grotty ,

1547
01:34:43,679 --> 01:34:47,975
and I think he established a whole separate world out there .

1548
01:34:48,141 --> 01:34:52,479
So it was a really interesting concept , I think .

1549
01:34:52,646 --> 01:34:55,565
And then when he stumbled , of course , onto the Giger stuff -

1550
01:34:55,732 --> 01:35:00,654
which has all the erotic sort of overtones to it -

1551
01:35:00,821 --> 01:35:05,659
it just sort of added to the whole thing .

1552
01:35:08,453 --> 01:35:12,666
( Scott ) So here we are in a new area , of which it wasn 't shown in the film .

1553
01:35:12,833 --> 01:35:14,960
A lot of people were asking " Well , what happened to it ? "

1554
01:35:15,127 --> 01:35:18,130
But we agonized over this . Have it in , have it out .

1555
01:35:18,297 --> 01:35:19,965
Did we need to see what was happening to them ?

1556
01:35:20,132 --> 01:35:24,344
Did we need to see - most importantly of all - the tragedy of her

1557
01:35:24,511 --> 01:35:31,476
having to incinerate all her old friends , colleagues , crewmates .

1558
01:35:31,935 --> 01:35:37,024
And we finally elected not to . We wanted to move on .

1559
01:35:37,316 --> 01:35:38,859
But she does it really well .

1560
01:35:39,318 --> 01:35:43,530
Completely both fearful and vulnerable at the same time .

1561
01:35:43,697 --> 01:35:45,782
But somehow in control .

1562
01:35:46,366 --> 01:35:48,785
Who would go in there at that point ?

1563
01:35:49,369 --> 01:35:53,832
If I mixed this , I 'd certainly have the echoes of the warning system going .

1564
01:35:53,999 --> 01:35:56,710
So she knows she 's against the clock .

1565
01:35:57,252 --> 01:35:59,713
This was a marvelous panel of Giger 's on the right ,

1566
01:36:01,131 --> 01:36:02,883
which was beautifully done .

1567
01:36:04,551 --> 01:36:09,264
And we get the fact that their morphing - metamorphosing -

1568
01:36:09,431 --> 01:36:14,436
they are changing into being consumed - I guess

1569
01:36:14,603 --> 01:36:17,939
by whatever the alien 's organism is - into an egg .

1570
01:36:21,777 --> 01:36:24,196
Nobody 'd seen this kind of thing .

1571
01:36:24,363 --> 01:36:27,574
That 's one of the difficulties . You now go again into this genre

1572
01:36:27,741 --> 01:36:34,122
and think of something that is equally unique and it 's difficult .

1573
01:36:57,270 --> 01:37:00,232
( Cartwright ) Alfred Hitchcock used to say " If you don 't see something ,

1574
01:37:00,399 --> 01:37:02,651
it 's your imagination . "

1575
01:37:02,818 --> 01:37:07,531
Cos I did The Birds , and there was this scene with the jungle gym

1576
01:37:07,697 --> 01:37:09,658
and all the birds on the jungle gym .

1577
01:37:09,825 --> 01:37:14,287
Well , a lot of them were cardboard and a lot of them were real .

1578
01:37:14,454 --> 01:37:16,164
I said " Well , aren 't people going to know ? "

1579
01:37:16,331 --> 01:37:18,667
And he says " Well , no . Your eye sees something

1580
01:37:18,834 --> 01:37:23,672
and it 's your imagination that makes you believe that everything is alive . "

1581
01:37:23,839 --> 01:37:26,049
And it 's the same thing with this alien .

1582
01:37:26,216 --> 01:37:29,678
I mean , you 're sitting there and after a while you start looking at the tubing and stuff

1583
01:37:29,845 --> 01:37:31,346
to see if it 's going to be coming out ,

1584
01:37:31,513 --> 01:37:34,975
and then all of sudden it comes out like this and you go " Shit , I didn 't see it there . "

1585
01:37:35,142 --> 01:37:40,063
I mean , it 's like it becomes so much more terrifying

1586
01:37:40,230 --> 01:37:43,108
than having something that you can blatantly see .

1587
01:37:43,275 --> 01:37:45,527
And I think that 's where the other ones

1588
01:37:45,694 --> 01:37:48,822
have sort of gone off and you just sort of lose interest .

1589
01:37:48,989 --> 01:37:52,826
I mean , because you 're not being a participant anymore .

1590
01:37:52,993 --> 01:37:56,455
You 're not using your imagination to create things .

1591
01:37:57,205 --> 01:37:59,499
I mean , this was the first time that there were people ,

1592
01:37:59,624 --> 01:38:03,712
where each character , somebody could get involved with .

1593
01:38:03,879 --> 01:38:06,631
And I was worried because I seemed to cry through the whole thing ,

1594
01:38:06,798 --> 01:38:10,010
and they said " No , you 're the audience . You 're the one who 's reasonable . "

1595
01:38:10,177 --> 01:38:12,637
" Let 's get , you know , out of here . "

1596
01:38:12,804 --> 01:38:17,058
So I guess it worked , obviously . I mean , they 've done four , so ...

1597
01:38:17,225 --> 01:38:20,479
I think it was probably a very good formula .

1598
01:38:20,645 --> 01:38:22,939
( Scott ) You know , the thing about a film like this is ,

1599
01:38:23,106 --> 01:38:28,737
this is a lot of perpetual demonstration of heart-stopping terror

1600
01:38:28,904 --> 01:38:33,783
because if the actor can 't give it to you , then the audience ain 't gonna feel it .

1601
01:38:33,950 --> 01:38:37,704
Right ? And eventually the wear-out factor on the beast will wear out .

1602
01:38:37,871 --> 01:38:40,332
That 's why I always kept it to minimal , like the shark .

1603
01:38:40,499 --> 01:38:43,084
- The less you see , the better . - ( Weaver ) Absolutely .

1604
01:38:43,251 --> 01:38:44,878
( Scott ) And so there 's a lot of stuff ...

1605
01:38:45,045 --> 01:38:48,507
And I 'll always remember , in watching the end of the big mix ,

1606
01:38:48,673 --> 01:38:52,010
thinking " Good God , there 's 17 and a half minutes at the end of this movie

1607
01:38:52,177 --> 01:38:57,307
where Sigourney has no dialogue , just a lot of physical stuff . "

1608
01:38:57,474 --> 01:39:01,770
" Running around in a constant state of catatonic terror . "

1609
01:39:01,937 --> 01:39:03,522
- Right ? - ( Weaver ) That 's my best thing .

1610
01:39:03,688 --> 01:39:04,731
( commentators laugh )

1611
01:39:04,898 --> 01:39:07,317
( Scott ) No , but it 's hard , isn 't it ?

1612
01:39:07,484 --> 01:39:09,194
( Weaver ) I loved that part of it . I loved ...

1613
01:39:09,361 --> 01:39:14,908
I loved the character , and just doing it all with images

1614
01:39:15,075 --> 01:39:19,204
because I think it emphasized her loneliness , her isolation .

1615
01:39:19,371 --> 01:39:22,541
And the fact that ... You know , she could have talked to her cat , I guess ,

1616
01:39:22,707 --> 01:39:27,254
but I loved that . I thought , she couldn 't even speak or communicate with anyone .

1617
01:39:27,420 --> 01:39:30,507
- It made her more vulnerable , I thought . - ( Scott ) Yeah , but it 's hard .

1618
01:39:30,632 --> 01:39:35,470
It 's difficult for ... As an outsider looking in - because that 's what my job is , to look in -

1619
01:39:37,597 --> 01:39:41,893
I always thought ... I was actually full of admiration for what you did ,

1620
01:39:42,060 --> 01:39:44,062
particularly through that whole process ,

1621
01:39:44,229 --> 01:39:49,276
cos that 's an area in the film that can easily become two-dimensional , right ?

1622
01:39:49,442 --> 01:39:53,905
And I think you got ... Somehow , you added two dimensions ,

1623
01:39:54,072 --> 01:39:58,702
so we had four dimensions going there . I always thought that was great .

1624
01:40:00,620 --> 01:40:02,998
I really like the performance of the light here as well

1625
01:40:03,164 --> 01:40:06,626
because you 're just using flat-on strobe lights here .

1626
01:40:06,793 --> 01:40:09,588
I 'd love to get stills of this actually .

1627
01:40:16,595 --> 01:40:20,307
( O 'Bannon ) It just ... It was like a bolt of lightning when I realized

1628
01:40:20,473 --> 01:40:23,268
that the audience didn 't have to be told .

1629
01:40:23,435 --> 01:40:31,443
What I realized then was that if it was difficult or artificial to tell ,

1630
01:40:31,610 --> 01:40:33,987
then it shouldn 't be told at all .

1631
01:40:34,154 --> 01:40:36,656
That the only things that the audience should be told ,

1632
01:40:36,823 --> 01:40:38,241
in terms of exposition ,

1633
01:40:38,408 --> 01:40:44,497
were things that were natural and easy for the characters to be speaking about .

1634
01:40:44,664 --> 01:40:49,085
And that if it was not natural and easy for them to be speaking about these things ,

1635
01:40:49,252 --> 01:40:52,088
then it shouldn 't be in there at all .

1636
01:40:52,255 --> 01:40:57,010
And this is a principle of exposition that I 've used ever since .

1637
01:40:57,177 --> 01:40:59,220
Not just in dialogue , but in general .

1638
01:40:59,387 --> 01:41:04,184
Any time that I find myself explaining something in a screenplay ,

1639
01:41:04,351 --> 01:41:10,106
and it seems forced or unnatural , that 's when I stop and say :

1640
01:41:10,273 --> 01:41:11,358
" Ah . "

1641
01:41:11,524 --> 01:41:15,695
" That 's because it doesn 't need to be told . It shouldn 't be told . "

1642
01:41:15,862 --> 01:41:21,493
If it belonged in the story , it would emerge naturally .

1643
01:41:21,660 --> 01:41:25,038
If it doesn 't emerge naturally , it shouldn 't be in there

1644
01:41:25,205 --> 01:41:27,624
and it 's just gonna sound horrible .

1645
01:41:27,791 --> 01:41:31,753
Didn 't matter what they were doing out there . Who gave a honk ?

1646
01:41:31,920 --> 01:41:36,299
It mattered to us , the filmmakers . It mattered to me , the writer ,

1647
01:41:36,466 --> 01:41:39,886
so that we could create a plausible world for these people ,

1648
01:41:40,053 --> 01:41:47,227
so that you could perceive them as real people in a real situation with a real history .

1649
01:41:50,522 --> 01:41:55,402
( Scott ) Now , the film was meant to be over when she goes inside .

1650
01:41:55,568 --> 01:41:58,571
You take off , you do the signing off .

1651
01:41:58,738 --> 01:42:01,700
Now , clearly , you cannot end the film here ,

1652
01:42:02,575 --> 01:42:04,119
even with a big bang behind her .

1653
01:42:04,285 --> 01:42:08,248
And , you know , the big bangs then are not the big bangs we could do today .

1654
01:42:08,415 --> 01:42:12,001
But you know what ? Today it 'd be CGI . This is all real .

1655
01:42:12,168 --> 01:42:16,423
Inside the studio , on the set ,

1656
01:42:16,589 --> 01:42:19,592
which is , you know , only firewood .

1657
01:42:20,719 --> 01:42:24,139
Okay , so this is all designed as the end of the movie .

1658
01:42:24,556 --> 01:42:27,809
So there 's a 1 - megaton thing gonna go off in a second .

1659
01:42:30,437 --> 01:42:32,313
And graphic design , interesting , huh ?

1660
01:42:32,480 --> 01:42:34,274
Just a flat card , nothing happening .

1661
01:42:34,441 --> 01:42:38,236
Just the sound and mixing between three cards .

1662
01:42:38,403 --> 01:42:42,198
And I figure you 've gotta have two or three .

1663
01:42:42,365 --> 01:42:45,118
I got the wobbling down now pretty good .

1664
01:42:45,869 --> 01:42:48,663
Nobody had really wobbled the camera till this moment , I remember that .

1665
01:42:48,830 --> 01:42:51,708
Something 's really wobbling the camera .

1666
01:42:58,006 --> 01:43:00,258
A bit unsure about the red ball .

1667
01:43:08,850 --> 01:43:10,518
And score .

1668
01:43:10,685 --> 01:43:15,273
Now , this would be the end of the film .

1669
01:43:16,274 --> 01:43:19,235
That 's it . She 'd do a signing off there .

1670
01:43:19,402 --> 01:43:24,866
And I had to say " No . You know , you can 't do that . It cannot possibly end there . "

1671
01:43:25,575 --> 01:43:28,536
You know , we made it for 8.6 , which even in those days was pretty cheap .

1672
01:43:28,703 --> 01:43:30,622
( Weaver ) I didn 't know that . I thought it was 14 .

1673
01:43:30,789 --> 01:43:32,499
- ( Scott ) Eight-point-six . - ( Weaver ) Wow .

1674
01:43:32,665 --> 01:43:34,083
( Scott ) Yeah . So at the end , I said :

1675
01:43:34,250 --> 01:43:37,796
" I wanna spend X to give you a fourth act . "

1676
01:43:37,962 --> 01:43:40,799
" How much ? " And I said " Well , it 'll take us four days . "

1677
01:43:40,965 --> 01:43:42,050
" Four days ? "

1678
01:43:42,217 --> 01:43:44,719
I said " Well , you don 't even know what I 'm gonna do yet . "

1679
01:43:44,886 --> 01:43:46,262
" Let me explain what I 'm gonna do . "

1680
01:43:46,429 --> 01:43:49,599
" Then you 'll go ' how can you do that in four days ? ' as opposed to ' four days ? " '

1681
01:43:49,766 --> 01:43:52,936
I said " We 're not finishing the film when she jumps in the shuttle . "

1682
01:43:53,102 --> 01:43:54,687
" There 's a fourth act . "

1683
01:43:54,854 --> 01:43:56,815
And they said " What do you mean ? "

1684
01:43:56,981 --> 01:44:00,193
" The film 's over when she jumps in the shuttle . It takes off , and bingo . "

1685
01:44:00,360 --> 01:44:06,533
I said " No , there 's a fourth act in there which will change the way films are made . "

1686
01:44:06,699 --> 01:44:11,704
Because I think , until this moment , it 's almost fair to say ...

1687
01:44:11,871 --> 01:44:13,998
There 's probably gonna be some small independent films

1688
01:44:14,165 --> 01:44:15,750
saying to me " You 're full of bull . "

1689
01:44:15,917 --> 01:44:19,712
But , you know , today we 've gotten into the idea now of a " the end , "

1690
01:44:19,879 --> 01:44:22,507
then " the end , " and then " the end . "

1691
01:44:22,674 --> 01:44:24,634
- And by the way , here 's the other end . - ( Weaver ) Yeah .

1692
01:44:24,801 --> 01:44:28,304
( Scott ) And I always wanted to close the lid and then suddenly let it out again .

1693
01:44:28,471 --> 01:44:29,681
- Ba-bam . Okay ? - ( Weaver ) Yes .

1694
01:44:29,848 --> 01:44:33,643
( Scott ) So when Ripley goes into that shuttle ,

1695
01:44:33,810 --> 01:44:35,895
there 's a moment where you know the film isn 't over .

1696
01:44:36,062 --> 01:44:39,607
When you 're sitting in the seat and the camera 's craning up

1697
01:44:39,774 --> 01:44:43,778
and Jerry Goldsmith does a really great little turn in the cue ,

1698
01:44:43,945 --> 01:44:45,280
and that 's where the screw turns .

1699
01:44:45,446 --> 01:44:47,615
And you can feel the whole audience , who are now ...

1700
01:44:47,782 --> 01:44:51,494
At this first-time screening , you could feel that they were like that .

1701
01:44:51,661 --> 01:44:55,790
You could feel that screw turning there in the lid , and you could feel people go :

1702
01:44:55,957 --> 01:44:57,792
" Oh , no . Please , God , let me out of here . "

1703
01:44:57,959 --> 01:45:00,587
- Which is a great thing to have going . - ( Weaver ) Yes , yes , yes .

1704
01:45:00,753 --> 01:45:03,923
Well , I loved all of that also . I 'd done a lot of dance so it really ...

1705
01:45:04,090 --> 01:45:08,803
I think so much of these movies is physical and sensory .

1706
01:45:09,345 --> 01:45:15,310
And , you know , to just be there with the smell of the smoke and everything .

1707
01:45:15,476 --> 01:45:19,480
- You know , it was so eerie and scary . - ( Scott ) Yeah , yeah .

1708
01:45:19,647 --> 01:45:23,359
( Weaver ) And I loved the music that you picked . What was the music ?

1709
01:45:23,526 --> 01:45:27,989
( Scott ) We used Tomita , cos I remember saying ... We talked about this on the phone .

1710
01:45:28,156 --> 01:45:33,661
Cos I was thinking " Whatever I can do right now , she 's on her own , " right ?

1711
01:45:34,162 --> 01:45:38,499
So I said , I 'd been playing with Tomita in the editing room as a temp track -

1712
01:45:38,666 --> 01:45:39,792
a temp score .

1713
01:45:39,959 --> 01:45:45,840
And he 'd done " Planets , " and one of them was " God of War is Mars . "

1714
01:45:46,007 --> 01:45:49,469
( Weaver ) Right , right , right . Which we used a lot in Alien 3 , I think .

1715
01:45:49,636 --> 01:45:51,679
( Scott ) And I said " Do you want ... ? "

1716
01:45:51,846 --> 01:45:54,849
I was very tentative about this , about suggesting it .

1717
01:45:55,016 --> 01:45:58,102
And I was really amazed when you said " Absolutely . "

1718
01:45:58,269 --> 01:45:59,938
" Anything like that would be brilliant . "

1719
01:46:00,104 --> 01:46:04,609
I said " Look , I can organize half a dozen 15-inch speakers down the side of the set ,

1720
01:46:04,776 --> 01:46:08,988
and I 've got this great piece of music which you may find extremely useful . "

1721
01:46:09,155 --> 01:46:15,328
" Cos not only does it sound like engines , it is extremely threatening and ominous . "

1722
01:46:15,495 --> 01:46:19,290
You said , " Anything like that . What is it ? Let me hear it . "

1723
01:46:19,457 --> 01:46:21,542
Bang , and we used that .

1724
01:46:21,709 --> 01:46:25,880
But it drove the sound guys crazy because everything had to be resounded .

1725
01:46:26,047 --> 01:46:28,257
- ( Weaver ) Oh , yeah . - ( Scott ) At that moment I didn 't care .

1726
01:46:28,424 --> 01:46:29,717
- ( Weaver ) No . - ( Scott ) I said " No . "

1727
01:46:29,884 --> 01:46:32,220
- ( Weaver ) There wasn 't much dialogue . - ( Scott ) No . Ha , ha , ha .

1728
01:46:32,387 --> 01:46:33,721
( Weaver chuckles )

1729
01:46:34,222 --> 01:46:38,726
( Weaver ) And I remember asking you not to tell me what was gonna happen

1730
01:46:38,893 --> 01:46:41,396
- so that I could be really surprised . - ( Scott ) Yeah , right .

1731
01:46:41,562 --> 01:46:43,648
( Weaver ) And I 've always felt that the reason ...

1732
01:46:43,815 --> 01:46:48,319
I mean , just casting , as you say , Bolaji Badejo , who 's that ...

1733
01:46:48,486 --> 01:46:52,407
You know , his arm ... My arm was like his leg .

1734
01:46:52,573 --> 01:46:55,910
I mean , he looked like he was from another world anyway ,

1735
01:46:56,077 --> 01:46:59,789
but when you put the suit on him , it was stunningly beautiful ,

1736
01:46:59,956 --> 01:47:02,083
as well as being terrifying .

1737
01:47:02,250 --> 01:47:06,921
And all you needed was that one gesture , and it was so scary .

1738
01:47:07,088 --> 01:47:09,257
( Scott ) We always wanted to make him intelligent .

1739
01:47:09,424 --> 01:47:14,012
( Weaver ) We actually wanted to have more of a sort of quasi-sex scene .

1740
01:47:14,178 --> 01:47:18,266
And who was the executive at Fox in London ?

1741
01:47:18,433 --> 01:47:20,601
- Peter ... ? - ( Scott ) Yeah , Peter Beale .

1742
01:47:20,768 --> 01:47:25,314
( Weaver ) Peter Beale would come on the set and give us a very stern Germanic look .

1743
01:47:25,481 --> 01:47:29,360
And kind of look at his watch , and basically said to you :

1744
01:47:29,527 --> 01:47:33,239
" You have two days to finish this , " or something .

1745
01:47:33,406 --> 01:47:35,241
And so this whole other thing ...

1746
01:47:35,408 --> 01:47:40,204
We wanted the alien to come and look at her through the glass

1747
01:47:40,371 --> 01:47:46,544
and be intrigued by the soft pinkness of her compared to him .

1748
01:47:46,711 --> 01:47:50,798
We wanted him to be that intelligent , and that it kind of turned him on .

1749
01:47:50,965 --> 01:47:52,759
( Scott ) Exactly . There was a moment that we wanted ...

1750
01:47:52,925 --> 01:47:54,927
( Weaver ) " Beauty and the Beast , " I think we were going for .

1751
01:47:55,094 --> 01:47:57,346
( Scott ) Exactly , right . Yeah .

1752
01:47:58,306 --> 01:48:03,644
( Weaver ) There is an appetite for a fifth one , which is something I never expected .

1753
01:48:03,811 --> 01:48:05,646
And , you know , I say :

1754
01:48:05,813 --> 01:48:12,779
" Well , it 's really hard to come up with a fifth story that 's new and fresh ,

1755
01:48:12,945 --> 01:48:17,325
but I have wanted to go back into space . "

1756
01:48:17,617 --> 01:48:21,204
I think outer-space adventure is a good thing for us right now

1757
01:48:21,370 --> 01:48:23,831
because Earth is so grim .

1758
01:48:24,999 --> 01:48:28,336
And so we 've been talking about it , but very generally .

1759
01:48:28,503 --> 01:48:32,465
( Scott ) Yeah . It 's a tough one , particularly with the success of 4 .

1760
01:48:32,632 --> 01:48:36,344
I think if you close the lid , it should be the end of the first chapter .

1761
01:48:36,511 --> 01:48:38,971
- ( Weaver ) Mm-hm . - ( Scott ) And I think , very simply ,

1762
01:48:39,138 --> 01:48:42,767
what no one 's done is simply gone back to revisit " What was it ? "

1763
01:48:42,934 --> 01:48:44,852
- No one 's ever said : - ( Weaver ) Where did it come from ?

1764
01:48:45,019 --> 01:48:47,480
( Scott ) " Who 's the space jockey ? He wasn 't an alien . "

1765
01:48:47,647 --> 01:48:49,440
- ( Weaver ) Yeah . - ( Scott ) " What was that battleship ? "

1766
01:48:49,607 --> 01:48:52,401
" Is it a battleship , is it an aircraft carrier ? "

1767
01:48:52,568 --> 01:48:59,909
" Is it a biomechanoid weapon carrier as opposed to an aircraft carrier ? "

1768
01:49:00,076 --> 01:49:02,203
" Why did it land ? Did it crashland

1769
01:49:02,370 --> 01:49:05,248
or did it settle there because it had engine trouble ? "

1770
01:49:05,414 --> 01:49:08,000
If those things have engines . Everything has an engine .

1771
01:49:08,167 --> 01:49:11,087
- ( Weaver ) Or did he get the SOS ? - ( Scott ) Exactly .

1772
01:49:11,254 --> 01:49:13,464
- And how long ago ? - ( Weaver ) Yes .

1773
01:49:13,631 --> 01:49:17,093
( Scott ) Cos those eggs would sit there .

1774
01:49:22,098 --> 01:49:27,687
( Scott ) Sigourney said " I feel I wanna sing something to keep me distracted . "

1775
01:49:28,146 --> 01:49:33,151
And she came up with " You Are My Lucky Star . "

1776
01:49:33,317 --> 01:49:35,194
I thought " What a great idea . "

1777
01:49:36,237 --> 01:49:39,073
And then the powers that be back at the studio said :

1778
01:49:39,240 --> 01:49:42,910
" Do you know how expensive 'You Are My Lucky Star ' is ? "

1779
01:49:43,077 --> 01:49:45,329
But that was it .

1780
01:49:45,496 --> 01:49:49,584
I think it 's nice , the idea of having her choosing to sing ,

1781
01:49:49,750 --> 01:49:54,088
almost as an iron bar , to hang onto her own sanity .

1782
01:49:54,422 --> 01:49:56,757
Can you imagine if this was real ?

1783
01:49:56,924 --> 01:50:00,303
I thought it was a good idea so we just did it .

1784
01:50:07,059 --> 01:50:10,563
( Stanton ) Yeah , there 's a lot of talented people involved in that .

1785
01:50:10,730 --> 01:50:13,107
Sigourney was great .

1786
01:50:13,858 --> 01:50:16,736
And that 's usually a man 's part ,

1787
01:50:16,903 --> 01:50:21,282
and to carry it off with this strength that she has - this presence -

1788
01:50:21,449 --> 01:50:24,285
that 's very impressive .

1789
01:50:24,452 --> 01:50:26,495
And a great statement for the women too .

1790
01:50:26,662 --> 01:50:27,997
( Stanton chuckles )

1791
01:50:28,748 --> 01:50:31,083
The women 's movement .

1792
01:50:37,340 --> 01:50:40,801
( Scott ) This is , again , a very good view of the alien .

1793
01:50:43,304 --> 01:50:47,141
I think you 've seen very little snippets of him up to now ,

1794
01:50:47,308 --> 01:50:51,354
but the danger is " Yes , he 's a man in a suit , " but then it would be .

1795
01:50:51,520 --> 01:50:55,983
It would be a humanoid version of an alien life form .

1796
01:51:00,279 --> 01:51:04,492
This is the most vulnerable moment for the way the alien looks .

1797
01:51:04,951 --> 01:51:06,285
Bang .

1798
01:51:06,452 --> 01:51:11,123
And that 's half the ship hanging upside down .

1799
